window.CET6_BANKS.push({"id": "2019-12-1", "source": "2019年12月真题·第1套", "writing": {"prompt": "Directions: For this part, you are allowed 30 minutes to write an essay on the importance of having a sense of social responsibility. You should write at least 150 words but no more than 200 words.", "sample": "In an era of rapid social change, a sense of social responsibility has become indispensable rather than\noptional. It refers to the awareness that our choices affect others and the willingness to act for the common\ngood. Far from being an empty moral slogan, it is the very foundation on which individuals grow and\ncommunities thrive.\nFirst and foremost, social responsibility drives personal growth. When students volunteer in nursing homes or\njoin campus clean-up campaigns, they cultivate empathy, discipline and leadership that no textbook can teach.\nWhat is more, it holds society together. During the pandemic, countless ordinary people delivered supplies and\nstaffed testing sites, and it was precisely this collective sense of duty that kept cities running. Without such\ncommitment, public trust would collapse and every individual would eventually pay the price.\nIn conclusion, a sense of social responsibility matters because it shapes better citizens and a more harmonious\nsociety. We should therefore start with small deeds—recycling, helping a neighbor, or speaking up for the\nvulnerable—and let responsibility become a habit rather than a duty."}, "translation": {"prompt": "牡丹花色艳丽，形象高雅，象征着和平与繁荣，因而在中国被称为\"花中之王\"。中国许多地方都培育和种植牡丹。千百年来，创造了许多诗歌和绘画赞美牡丹。唐代时期，牡丹在皇家园林普遍种植并被誉为国花，因而特别风行。十世纪时，洛阳古城成为牡丹栽培中心，而且这一地位一直保持到今天。现在，成千上万的国内游客蜂拥到洛阳参加一年一度的牡丹节，欣赏洛阳牡丹独特之美，同时探索九朝古都的历史。", "sample": "The peony is bright in color and elegant in appearance, and it symbolizes peace and prosperity, so it is known as \"the king of flowers\" in China. Peonies are cultivated and grown in many parts of China. Over the centuries, many poems and paintings have been created to praise the peony. During the Tang Dynasty, peonies were widely planted in royal gardens and honored as the national flower, thus becoming especially popular. In the tenth century, the ancient city of Luoyang became the center of peony cultivation, a status it has maintained to this day. Today, thousands of domestic tourists flock to Luoyang to attend the annual Peony Festival, appreciate the unique beauty of Luoyang's peonies, and explore the history of the ancient capital of nine dynasties."}, "listeningUrl": "https://english-exam.lazynote.cn/cet6/paper/2019-12-1/?f=w", "clozePassage": "When considering risk factors associated with serious chronic diseases, we often think about health indicators such as cholesterol, blood pressure, and body weight. But poor diet and physical inactivity also each increase the risk of heart disease and have a role to play in the development of some cancers. Perhaps worse, the  __26__  effects of an unhealthy diet and insufficient exercise are not limited to your body. Recent research has also shown that  __27__  in a high-fat and high-sugar diet may have negative effects on your brain, causing learning and memory  __28__  .\nStudies have found obesity is associated with impairments in cognitive functioning, as  __29__  by a range of learning and memory tests, such as the ability to remember a list of words presented some minutes or hours earlier. There is also a growing body of evidence that diet-induced cognitive impairments can emerge  __30__  —within weeks or even days. For example, one study found healthy adults  __31__  to a high-fat diet for five days showed impaired attention, memory, and mood compared with a low-fat diet control group. Another study also found eating a high-fat and high-sugar breakfast each day for as little as four days resulted in problems with learning and memory  __32__  to those observed in overweight and obese individuals.\nBody weight was not hugely different between the groups eating a healthy diet and those on high fat and sugar diets. So this shows negative  __33__  of poor dietary intake can occur even when body weight has not changed  __34__  . Thus, body weight is not always the best indicator of health and a thin person still needs to eat well and exercise  __35__  .", "matchIntro": "Increased Screen Time and Wellbeing Decline in Youth", "matchParagraphs": [{"label": "A", "text": "Have young people never had it so good? Or do they face more challenges than any previous generation? Our current era in the West is one of high wealth. This means minors enjoy material benefits and legal protections that would have been the envy of those living in the past. But there is an increasing suspicion that all is not well for our youth. And one of the most popular explanations, among some experts and the popular media, is that excessive \"screen time\" is to blame. (This refers to all the attention young people devote to their phones, tablets and laptops.) However, this is a contentious theory and such claims have been treated skeptically by some scholars based on their reading of the relevant data."}, {"label": "B", "text": "Now a new study has provided another contribution to the debate, uncovering strong evidence that adolescent wellbeing in the United States really is experiencing a decline and arguing that the most likely cause is the electronic riches we have given them. The background to this is that from the 1960s into the early 2000s, measures of average wellbeing went up in the US. This was especially true for younger people. It reflected the fact that these decades saw a climb in general standards of living and avoidance of mass societal traumas like full-scale war or economic deprivation. However, the \"screen time\" hypothesis, advanced by researchers such as Jean Twenge, is that electronic devices and excessive time spent online may have reversed these trends in recent years, causing problems for young people's psychological health."}, {"label": "C", "text": "To investigate, Twenge and her colleagues dived into the \"Monitoring the Future\" dataset based on annual surveys of American school students from grades 8, 10, and 12 that started in 1991. In total, 1.1 million young people answered various questions related to their wellbeing. Twenge's team's analysis of the answers confirmed the earlier, well-established wellbeing climb, with scores rising across the 1990s, and into the later 2000s. This was found across measures like self-esteem, life satisfaction, happiness and satisfaction with individual domains like job, neighborhood, or friends. But around 2012 these measures started to decline. This continued through 2016, the most recent year for which data is available."}, {"label": "D", "text": "Twenge and her colleagues wanted to understand why this change in average wellbeing occurred. However, it is very hard to demonstrate causes using non-experimental data such as this. In fact, when Twenge previously used this data to suggest a screen time effect, some commentators were quick to raise this problem. They argued that her causal-sounding claims rested on correlational data, and that she had not adequately accounted for other potential causal factors. This time around, Twenge and her team make a point of saying that they are not trying to establish causes as such, but that they are assessing the plausibility of potential causes."}, {"label": "E", "text": "First, they explain that if a given variable is playing a role in affecting wellbeing, then we should expect any change in that variable to correlate with the observed changes in wellbeing. If not, it is not plausible that the variable is a causal factor. So the researchers looked at time spent in a number of activities that could plausibly be driving the wellbeing decline. Less sport, and fewer meetings with peers correlated with lower wellbeing, as did less time reading print media (newspapers) and, surprisingly, less time doing homework. (This last finding would appear to contradict another popular hypothesis that it is our burdening of students with assignments that is causing all the problems.) In addition, more TV watching and more electronic communication both correlated with lower wellbeing. All these effects held true for measures of happiness, life satisfaction and self-esteem, with the effects stronger in the 8th and 10th-graders."}, {"label": "F", "text": "Next, Twenge's team dug a little deeper into the data on screen time. They found that adolescents who spent a very small amount of time on digital devices—a couple of hours a week—had the highest wellbeing. Their wellbeing was even higher than those who never used such devices. However, higher doses of screen time were clearly associated with lower happiness. Those spending 10-19 hours per week on their devices were 41 percent more likely to be unhappy than lower-frequency users. Those who used such devices 40 hours a week or more (one in ten teenagers) were twice as likely to be unhappy. The data was slightly complicated by the fact that there was a tendency for kids who were social in the real world to also use more online communication, but by bracketing out different cases it became clear that the real-world sociality component correlated with greater wellbeing, whereas greater time on screens or online only correlated with poorer wellbeing."}, {"label": "G", "text": "So far, so plausible. But the next question is, are the drops in average wellbeing happening at the same time trends toward increased electronic device usage? It looks like it—after all, 2012 was the tipping point when more than half of Americans began owning smartphones. Twenge and her colleagues also found that across the key years of 2013-16, wellbeing was indeed lowest in years where adolescents spent more time online, on social media, and reading news online, and when more youth in the United States had smartphones. And in a second analysis, they found that where technology went, dips in wellbeing followed. For instance, years with a larger increase in online usage were followed by years with lower wellbeing, rather than the other way around. This does not prove causality, but is consistent with it. Meanwhile, TV use did not show this tracking. TV might make you less happy, but this is not what seems to be driving the recent declines in young people's average happiness."}, {"label": "H", "text": "A similar but reversed pattern was found for the activities associated with greater wellbeing. For example, years when people spent more time with friends were better years for wellbeing (and followed by better years). Sadly, the data also showed face-to-face socializing and sports activity had declined over the period covered by the survey."}, {"label": "I", "text": "There is another explanation that Twenge and her colleagues wanted to address: the impact of the great recession of 2007-2009, which hit a great number of American families and might be affecting adolescents. The dataset they used did not include economic data, so instead the researchers looked at whether the 2013-16 wellbeing decline was tracking economic indicators. They found some evidence that some crude measures, like income inequality, correlated with changes in wellbeing, but economic measures with a more direct impact, like family income and unemployment rates (which put families into difficulties), had no relationship with wellbeing. The researchers also note that the recession hit some years before we see the beginning of the wellbeing drop, and before the steepest wellbeing decline, which occurred in 2013."}, {"label": "J", "text": "The researchers conclude that electronic communication was the only adolescent activity that increased at the same time psychological wellbeing declined. I suspect that some experts in the field will be keen to address alternative explanations, such as unassessed variables playing a role in the wellbeing decline. But the new work does go further than previous research and suggests that screen time should still be considered a potential barrier to young people's flourishing."}], "readingPassages": {"46": "\"The dangerous thing about lying is people don't understand how the act changes us,\" says Dan Ariely behavioural psychologist at Duke University. Psychologists have documented children lying as early as the age of two. Some experts even consider lying a developmental milestone, like crawling and walking, because it requires sophisticated planning, attention and the ability to see a situation from someone else's perspective to manipulate them. But, for most people, lying gets limited as we develop a sense of morality and the ability to self-regulate.\nHarvard cognitive neuroscientist Joshua Greene says, for most of us, lying takes work. In studies, he gave subjects a chance to deceive for monetary gain while examining their brains in a functional MRI machine, which maps blood flow to active parts of the brain. Some people told the truth instantly and instinctively. But others opted to lie, and they showed increased activity in their frontal parietal(颅腔壁的) control network, which is involved in difficult or complex thinking. This suggests that they were deciding between truth and dishonesty—and ultimately opting for the latter. For a follow-up analysis, he found that people whose neural(神经的) reward centers were more active when they won money were also more likely to be among the group of liars – suggesting that lying may have to do with the inability to resist temptation.\nExternal conditions also matter in terms of when and how often we lie. We are more likely to lie, when we are able to rationalise it, when we are stressed and fatigued or see others being dishonest. And we are less likely to lie when we have moral reminders or when we think others are watching. \"We as a society need to understand that, when we don't punish lying, we increase the probability it will happen again,\" Ariely says.\nIn a 2016 study published in the journal Nature Neuroscience, Ariely and colleagues showed how dishonesty alters people's brains, making it easier to tell lies in the future. When people uttered a falsehood, the scientists noticed a burst of activity in their amygdala. The amygdala is a crucial part of the brain that produces fear, anxiety and emotional responses including that sinking, guilty feeling you get when you lie. But when scientists had their subjects play a game—in which they won money by deceiving their partner, they noticed the negative signals from the amygdala began to decrease. Not only that, but when people faced no consequences for dishonesty, their falsehood tended to get even more sensational. This means that if you give people multiple opportunities to lie for their own benefit, they start with little lies which get bigger over time.", "47": "\"The dangerous thing about lying is people don't understand how the act changes us,\" says Dan Ariely behavioural psychologist at Duke University. Psychologists have documented children lying as early as the age of two. Some experts even consider lying a developmental milestone, like crawling and walking, because it requires sophisticated planning, attention and the ability to see a situation from someone else's perspective to manipulate them. But, for most people, lying gets limited as we develop a sense of morality and the ability to self-regulate.\nHarvard cognitive neuroscientist Joshua Greene says, for most of us, lying takes work. In studies, he gave subjects a chance to deceive for monetary gain while examining their brains in a functional MRI machine, which maps blood flow to active parts of the brain. Some people told the truth instantly and instinctively. But others opted to lie, and they showed increased activity in their frontal parietal(颅腔壁的) control network, which is involved in difficult or complex thinking. This suggests that they were deciding between truth and dishonesty—and ultimately opting for the latter. For a follow-up analysis, he found that people whose neural(神经的) reward centers were more active when they won money were also more likely to be among the group of liars – suggesting that lying may have to do with the inability to resist temptation.\nExternal conditions also matter in terms of when and how often we lie. We are more likely to lie, when we are able to rationalise it, when we are stressed and fatigued or see others being dishonest. And we are less likely to lie when we have moral reminders or when we think others are watching. \"We as a society need to understand that, when we don't punish lying, we increase the probability it will happen again,\" Ariely says.\nIn a 2016 study published in the journal Nature Neuroscience, Ariely and colleagues showed how dishonesty alters people's brains, making it easier to tell lies in the future. When people uttered a falsehood, the scientists noticed a burst of activity in their amygdala. The amygdala is a crucial part of the brain that produces fear, anxiety and emotional responses including that sinking, guilty feeling you get when you lie. But when scientists had their subjects play a game—in which they won money by deceiving their partner, they noticed the negative signals from the amygdala began to decrease. Not only that, but when people faced no consequences for dishonesty, their falsehood tended to get even more sensational. This means that if you give people multiple opportunities to lie for their own benefit, they start with little lies which get bigger over time.", "48": "\"The dangerous thing about lying is people don't understand how the act changes us,\" says Dan Ariely behavioural psychologist at Duke University. Psychologists have documented children lying as early as the age of two. Some experts even consider lying a developmental milestone, like crawling and walking, because it requires sophisticated planning, attention and the ability to see a situation from someone else's perspective to manipulate them. But, for most people, lying gets limited as we develop a sense of morality and the ability to self-regulate.\nHarvard cognitive neuroscientist Joshua Greene says, for most of us, lying takes work. In studies, he gave subjects a chance to deceive for monetary gain while examining their brains in a functional MRI machine, which maps blood flow to active parts of the brain. Some people told the truth instantly and instinctively. But others opted to lie, and they showed increased activity in their frontal parietal(颅腔壁的) control network, which is involved in difficult or complex thinking. This suggests that they were deciding between truth and dishonesty—and ultimately opting for the latter. For a follow-up analysis, he found that people whose neural(神经的) reward centers were more active when they won money were also more likely to be among the group of liars – suggesting that lying may have to do with the inability to resist temptation.\nExternal conditions also matter in terms of when and how often we lie. We are more likely to lie, when we are able to rationalise it, when we are stressed and fatigued or see others being dishonest. And we are less likely to lie when we have moral reminders or when we think others are watching. \"We as a society need to understand that, when we don't punish lying, we increase the probability it will happen again,\" Ariely says.\nIn a 2016 study published in the journal Nature Neuroscience, Ariely and colleagues showed how dishonesty alters people's brains, making it easier to tell lies in the future. When people uttered a falsehood, the scientists noticed a burst of activity in their amygdala. The amygdala is a crucial part of the brain that produces fear, anxiety and emotional responses including that sinking, guilty feeling you get when you lie. But when scientists had their subjects play a game—in which they won money by deceiving their partner, they noticed the negative signals from the amygdala began to decrease. Not only that, but when people faced no consequences for dishonesty, their falsehood tended to get even more sensational. This means that if you give people multiple opportunities to lie for their own benefit, they start with little lies which get bigger over time.", "49": "\"The dangerous thing about lying is people don't understand how the act changes us,\" says Dan Ariely behavioural psychologist at Duke University. Psychologists have documented children lying as early as the age of two. Some experts even consider lying a developmental milestone, like crawling and walking, because it requires sophisticated planning, attention and the ability to see a situation from someone else's perspective to manipulate them. But, for most people, lying gets limited as we develop a sense of morality and the ability to self-regulate.\nHarvard cognitive neuroscientist Joshua Greene says, for most of us, lying takes work. In studies, he gave subjects a chance to deceive for monetary gain while examining their brains in a functional MRI machine, which maps blood flow to active parts of the brain. Some people told the truth instantly and instinctively. But others opted to lie, and they showed increased activity in their frontal parietal(颅腔壁的) control network, which is involved in difficult or complex thinking. This suggests that they were deciding between truth and dishonesty—and ultimately opting for the latter. For a follow-up analysis, he found that people whose neural(神经的) reward centers were more active when they won money were also more likely to be among the group of liars – suggesting that lying may have to do with the inability to resist temptation.\nExternal conditions also matter in terms of when and how often we lie. We are more likely to lie, when we are able to rationalise it, when we are stressed and fatigued or see others being dishonest. And we are less likely to lie when we have moral reminders or when we think others are watching. \"We as a society need to understand that, when we don't punish lying, we increase the probability it will happen again,\" Ariely says.\nIn a 2016 study published in the journal Nature Neuroscience, Ariely and colleagues showed how dishonesty alters people's brains, making it easier to tell lies in the future. When people uttered a falsehood, the scientists noticed a burst of activity in their amygdala. The amygdala is a crucial part of the brain that produces fear, anxiety and emotional responses including that sinking, guilty feeling you get when you lie. But when scientists had their subjects play a game—in which they won money by deceiving their partner, they noticed the negative signals from the amygdala began to decrease. Not only that, but when people faced no consequences for dishonesty, their falsehood tended to get even more sensational. This means that if you give people multiple opportunities to lie for their own benefit, they start with little lies which get bigger over time.", "50": "\"The dangerous thing about lying is people don't understand how the act changes us,\" says Dan Ariely behavioural psychologist at Duke University. Psychologists have documented children lying as early as the age of two. Some experts even consider lying a developmental milestone, like crawling and walking, because it requires sophisticated planning, attention and the ability to see a situation from someone else's perspective to manipulate them. But, for most people, lying gets limited as we develop a sense of morality and the ability to self-regulate.\nHarvard cognitive neuroscientist Joshua Greene says, for most of us, lying takes work. In studies, he gave subjects a chance to deceive for monetary gain while examining their brains in a functional MRI machine, which maps blood flow to active parts of the brain. Some people told the truth instantly and instinctively. But others opted to lie, and they showed increased activity in their frontal parietal(颅腔壁的) control network, which is involved in difficult or complex thinking. This suggests that they were deciding between truth and dishonesty—and ultimately opting for the latter. For a follow-up analysis, he found that people whose neural(神经的) reward centers were more active when they won money were also more likely to be among the group of liars – suggesting that lying may have to do with the inability to resist temptation.\nExternal conditions also matter in terms of when and how often we lie. We are more likely to lie, when we are able to rationalise it, when we are stressed and fatigued or see others being dishonest. And we are less likely to lie when we have moral reminders or when we think others are watching. \"We as a society need to understand that, when we don't punish lying, we increase the probability it will happen again,\" Ariely says.\nIn a 2016 study published in the journal Nature Neuroscience, Ariely and colleagues showed how dishonesty alters people's brains, making it easier to tell lies in the future. When people uttered a falsehood, the scientists noticed a burst of activity in their amygdala. The amygdala is a crucial part of the brain that produces fear, anxiety and emotional responses including that sinking, guilty feeling you get when you lie. But when scientists had their subjects play a game—in which they won money by deceiving their partner, they noticed the negative signals from the amygdala began to decrease. Not only that, but when people faced no consequences for dishonesty, their falsehood tended to get even more sensational. This means that if you give people multiple opportunities to lie for their own benefit, they start with little lies which get bigger over time.", "51": "Here's how the Pacific Northwest is preparing for \"The Big One\". It's the mother of all disaster drills for what could be the worst disaster in American history. California has spent years preparing for \"The Big One\"—the inevitable earthquake that will undoubtedly unleash all kinds of havoc along the famous San Andreas fault(断层). But what if the fault that runs along the Pacific Northwest delivers a gigantic earthquake of its own? If the people of the Cascadia region have anything to do with it, they won't be caught unawares.\nThe region is engaged in a multi-day earthquake-and-tsunami(海啸) drill involving around 20,000 people. The Cascadia Rising drill gives area residents and emergency responders a chance to practice what to do in case of a 9.0 magnitude earthquake and tsunami along one of the nation's dangerous—and underestimated—faults.\nThe Cascadia Earthquake Zone is big enough to compete with San Andreas (it's been called the most dangerous fault in America), but it's much lesser known than its California cousin. Nearly 700 miles long, the earthquake zone is located by the North American Plate off the coast of Pacific British Columbia, Washington, Oregon and Northern California.\nCascadia is what's known as a \"megathrust\" fault. Megathrusts are created in earthquake zones—land plate boundaries where two plates converge. In the areas where one plate is beneath another, stress builds up over time. During a megathrust event, all of that stress releases and some of the world's most powerful earthquakes occur. Remember the 9.1 earthquake and tsunami in the Indian Ocean off Sumatra in 2004? It was caused by a megathrust event as the India plate moved beneath the Burma micro-plate.\nThe last time a major earthquake occurred along the Cascadia fault was in 1700, so officials worry that another event could occur any time. To prevent that event from becoming a catastrophe, first responders will join members of the public in rehearsals that involve communication, evacuation, search and rescue, and other scenarios.\nThousands of casualties are expected if a 9.0 earthquake were to occur. First, the earthquake would shake metropolitan areas including Seattle and Portland. This could trigger a tsunami that would create havoc along the coast. Not all casualties can necessarily be prevented—but by coordinating across local, state, and even national borders, officials hope that the worst-case scenario can be averted. On the exercise's website, officials explain that the report they prepare during this rehearsal will inform disaster management for years to come.\nFor hundreds of thousands of Cascadia residents, \"The Big One\" isn't a question of if, only when. And it's never too early to get ready for the inevitable.", "52": "Here's how the Pacific Northwest is preparing for \"The Big One\". It's the mother of all disaster drills for what could be the worst disaster in American history. California has spent years preparing for \"The Big One\"—the inevitable earthquake that will undoubtedly unleash all kinds of havoc along the famous San Andreas fault(断层). But what if the fault that runs along the Pacific Northwest delivers a gigantic earthquake of its own? If the people of the Cascadia region have anything to do with it, they won't be caught unawares.\nThe region is engaged in a multi-day earthquake-and-tsunami(海啸) drill involving around 20,000 people. The Cascadia Rising drill gives area residents and emergency responders a chance to practice what to do in case of a 9.0 magnitude earthquake and tsunami along one of the nation's dangerous—and underestimated—faults.\nThe Cascadia Earthquake Zone is big enough to compete with San Andreas (it's been called the most dangerous fault in America), but it's much lesser known than its California cousin. Nearly 700 miles long, the earthquake zone is located by the North American Plate off the coast of Pacific British Columbia, Washington, Oregon and Northern California.\nCascadia is what's known as a \"megathrust\" fault. Megathrusts are created in earthquake zones—land plate boundaries where two plates converge. In the areas where one plate is beneath another, stress builds up over time. During a megathrust event, all of that stress releases and some of the world's most powerful earthquakes occur. Remember the 9.1 earthquake and tsunami in the Indian Ocean off Sumatra in 2004? It was caused by a megathrust event as the India plate moved beneath the Burma micro-plate.\nThe last time a major earthquake occurred along the Cascadia fault was in 1700, so officials worry that another event could occur any time. To prevent that event from becoming a catastrophe, first responders will join members of the public in rehearsals that involve communication, evacuation, search and rescue, and other scenarios.\nThousands of casualties are expected if a 9.0 earthquake were to occur. First, the earthquake would shake metropolitan areas including Seattle and Portland. This could trigger a tsunami that would create havoc along the coast. Not all casualties can necessarily be prevented—but by coordinating across local, state, and even national borders, officials hope that the worst-case scenario can be averted. On the exercise's website, officials explain that the report they prepare during this rehearsal will inform disaster management for years to come.\nFor hundreds of thousands of Cascadia residents, \"The Big One\" isn't a question of if, only when. And it's never too early to get ready for the inevitable.", "53": "Here's how the Pacific Northwest is preparing for \"The Big One\". It's the mother of all disaster drills for what could be the worst disaster in American history. California has spent years preparing for \"The Big One\"—the inevitable earthquake that will undoubtedly unleash all kinds of havoc along the famous San Andreas fault(断层). But what if the fault that runs along the Pacific Northwest delivers a gigantic earthquake of its own? If the people of the Cascadia region have anything to do with it, they won't be caught unawares.\nThe region is engaged in a multi-day earthquake-and-tsunami(海啸) drill involving around 20,000 people. The Cascadia Rising drill gives area residents and emergency responders a chance to practice what to do in case of a 9.0 magnitude earthquake and tsunami along one of the nation's dangerous—and underestimated—faults.\nThe Cascadia Earthquake Zone is big enough to compete with San Andreas (it's been called the most dangerous fault in America), but it's much lesser known than its California cousin. Nearly 700 miles long, the earthquake zone is located by the North American Plate off the coast of Pacific British Columbia, Washington, Oregon and Northern California.\nCascadia is what's known as a \"megathrust\" fault. Megathrusts are created in earthquake zones—land plate boundaries where two plates converge. In the areas where one plate is beneath another, stress builds up over time. During a megathrust event, all of that stress releases and some of the world's most powerful earthquakes occur. Remember the 9.1 earthquake and tsunami in the Indian Ocean off Sumatra in 2004? It was caused by a megathrust event as the India plate moved beneath the Burma micro-plate.\nThe last time a major earthquake occurred along the Cascadia fault was in 1700, so officials worry that another event could occur any time. To prevent that event from becoming a catastrophe, first responders will join members of the public in rehearsals that involve communication, evacuation, search and rescue, and other scenarios.\nThousands of casualties are expected if a 9.0 earthquake were to occur. First, the earthquake would shake metropolitan areas including Seattle and Portland. This could trigger a tsunami that would create havoc along the coast. Not all casualties can necessarily be prevented—but by coordinating across local, state, and even national borders, officials hope that the worst-case scenario can be averted. On the exercise's website, officials explain that the report they prepare during this rehearsal will inform disaster management for years to come.\nFor hundreds of thousands of Cascadia residents, \"The Big One\" isn't a question of if, only when. And it's never too early to get ready for the inevitable.", "54": "Here's how the Pacific Northwest is preparing for \"The Big One\". It's the mother of all disaster drills for what could be the worst disaster in American history. California has spent years preparing for \"The Big One\"—the inevitable earthquake that will undoubtedly unleash all kinds of havoc along the famous San Andreas fault(断层). But what if the fault that runs along the Pacific Northwest delivers a gigantic earthquake of its own? If the people of the Cascadia region have anything to do with it, they won't be caught unawares.\nThe region is engaged in a multi-day earthquake-and-tsunami(海啸) drill involving around 20,000 people. The Cascadia Rising drill gives area residents and emergency responders a chance to practice what to do in case of a 9.0 magnitude earthquake and tsunami along one of the nation's dangerous—and underestimated—faults.\nThe Cascadia Earthquake Zone is big enough to compete with San Andreas (it's been called the most dangerous fault in America), but it's much lesser known than its California cousin. Nearly 700 miles long, the earthquake zone is located by the North American Plate off the coast of Pacific British Columbia, Washington, Oregon and Northern California.\nCascadia is what's known as a \"megathrust\" fault. Megathrusts are created in earthquake zones—land plate boundaries where two plates converge. In the areas where one plate is beneath another, stress builds up over time. During a megathrust event, all of that stress releases and some of the world's most powerful earthquakes occur. Remember the 9.1 earthquake and tsunami in the Indian Ocean off Sumatra in 2004? It was caused by a megathrust event as the India plate moved beneath the Burma micro-plate.\nThe last time a major earthquake occurred along the Cascadia fault was in 1700, so officials worry that another event could occur any time. To prevent that event from becoming a catastrophe, first responders will join members of the public in rehearsals that involve communication, evacuation, search and rescue, and other scenarios.\nThousands of casualties are expected if a 9.0 earthquake were to occur. First, the earthquake would shake metropolitan areas including Seattle and Portland. This could trigger a tsunami that would create havoc along the coast. Not all casualties can necessarily be prevented—but by coordinating across local, state, and even national borders, officials hope that the worst-case scenario can be averted. On the exercise's website, officials explain that the report they prepare during this rehearsal will inform disaster management for years to come.\nFor hundreds of thousands of Cascadia residents, \"The Big One\" isn't a question of if, only when. And it's never too early to get ready for the inevitable.", "55": "Here's how the Pacific Northwest is preparing for \"The Big One\". It's the mother of all disaster drills for what could be the worst disaster in American history. California has spent years preparing for \"The Big One\"—the inevitable earthquake that will undoubtedly unleash all kinds of havoc along the famous San Andreas fault(断层). But what if the fault that runs along the Pacific Northwest delivers a gigantic earthquake of its own? If the people of the Cascadia region have anything to do with it, they won't be caught unawares.\nThe region is engaged in a multi-day earthquake-and-tsunami(海啸) drill involving around 20,000 people. The Cascadia Rising drill gives area residents and emergency responders a chance to practice what to do in case of a 9.0 magnitude earthquake and tsunami along one of the nation's dangerous—and underestimated—faults.\nThe Cascadia Earthquake Zone is big enough to compete with San Andreas (it's been called the most dangerous fault in America), but it's much lesser known than its California cousin. Nearly 700 miles long, the earthquake zone is located by the North American Plate off the coast of Pacific British Columbia, Washington, Oregon and Northern California.\nCascadia is what's known as a \"megathrust\" fault. Megathrusts are created in earthquake zones—land plate boundaries where two plates converge. In the areas where one plate is beneath another, stress builds up over time. During a megathrust event, all of that stress releases and some of the world's most powerful earthquakes occur. Remember the 9.1 earthquake and tsunami in the Indian Ocean off Sumatra in 2004? It was caused by a megathrust event as the India plate moved beneath the Burma micro-plate.\nThe last time a major earthquake occurred along the Cascadia fault was in 1700, so officials worry that another event could occur any time. To prevent that event from becoming a catastrophe, first responders will join members of the public in rehearsals that involve communication, evacuation, search and rescue, and other scenarios.\nThousands of casualties are expected if a 9.0 earthquake were to occur. First, the earthquake would shake metropolitan areas including Seattle and Portland. This could trigger a tsunami that would create havoc along the coast. Not all casualties can necessarily be prevented—but by coordinating across local, state, and even national borders, officials hope that the worst-case scenario can be averted. On the exercise's website, officials explain that the report they prepare during this rehearsal will inform disaster management for years to come.\nFor hundreds of thousands of Cascadia residents, \"The Big One\" isn't a question of if, only when. And it's never too early to get ready for the inevitable."}, "questions": [{"id": "2019-12-1-l-1", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 1, "stem": "What is the woman's profession?", "options": ["A) Magazine reporter", "B) Fashion designer", "C) Website designer", "D) Features editor"], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） Features editor\n【定位】Today our guest is Rosie Mullender, who works as Features Editor for Fashion magazine.\n今天我们的嘉宾是罗西·穆兰德，她在《时尚》杂志担任专题编辑。\n【信号】主持人开场介绍即报职务。答案信号是开场白里主持人直接介绍嘉宾身份：who works as\nFeatures Editor for Fashion magazine，works as 后面紧跟的就是职务名，是身份题最稳\n的落点。\n【替换】works as Features Editor ↔ Features editor\n职务名几乎原样对应\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\n录音确实念到 Fashion magazine，选项 Magazine reporter 借这个\"杂志\"原词诱你选，但\n她是\"编辑 editor\"不是\"记者 reporter\"，职务对不上。\n借用原文：Fashion magazine\nB) 原词陷阱\n录音里 Fashion 是杂志的名字（Fashion magazine），选项 Fashion designer 借这个\nFashion 让你误以为她是\"时尚设计师\"，但她的工作是当编辑，不是做服装设计。\n借用原文：Fashion magazine\nC) 原词陷阱\n录音后面提到为杂志和网站写稿 writing copy for the magazine and website，选项\nWebsite designer 借这个 website 诱选，但\"为网站写稿\"不等于\"网站设计师\"，职务完全\n两回事。\n借用原文：writing copy for the magazine and website", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2019-12-1-l-2", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 2, "stem": "What is one of the woman's main responsibilities?", "options": ["A) Designing sports clothing", "B) Consulting fashion experts", "C) Answering daily emails", "D) Interviewing job-seekers"], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） Answering daily emails\n【定位】My day mainly incorporates responding to emails, writing and editing stories and coming up\nwith new feature ideas.\n我一天的工作主要包括回复邮件、撰写和编辑稿件，以及构思新的专题点子。\n【信号】mainly 标记主要职责。答案信号是被问者应答里的 My day mainly incorporates\nresponding to emails，mainly（主要）一词直接对应题干的 main responsibilities，并列\n第一项就是\"回复邮件\"。\n【替换】responding to emails ↔ Answering daily emails\nresponding=answering 的同义替换\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\n录音确有 testing sports clothing（测试运动服装），选项 Designing sports clothing\n借\"运动服装\"原词诱选，但她是\"测试 testing\"不是\"设计 designing\"，而且那只是偶尔做的\n零散事、不是\"主要\"职责。\n借用原文：testing sports clothing\nB) 未提及\n录音多次出现 fashion（杂志名、领域），选项 Consulting fashion experts 借这个\nfashion 拼出\"咨询时尚专家\"，但录音从未说她要咨询时尚专家，属编造的职责。\nD) 偷换概念\n录音说 asking people in the street questions（在街上问路人问题），选项 Interviewing\njob-seekers 把它歪成\"采访求职者\"，对象从\"路人\"换成\"求职者\"，对不上。\n借用原文：asking people in the street questions", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2019-12-1-l-3", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 3, "stem": "What do many people think about the woman's job?", "options": ["A) It is challenging.", "B) It is fascinating.", "C) It is tiresome.", "D) It is fashionable."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （态度观点题） It is fascinating.\n【定位】A lot of people believe that working at a magazine is a glamorous job. Is this an accurate\nrepresentation of what you do?\n很多人认为在杂志社工作是一份光鲜亮丽的工作。这是对你工作的准确写照吗？\n【信号】A lot of people believe 锁定大众看法。答案信号是主持人提问里的 A lot of people\nbelieve that working at a magazine is a glamorous job，A lot of people believe 直接\n对应题干 many people think，其后的 glamorous（光鲜迷人）就是大众的看法。\n【替换】a glamorous job ↔ It is fascinating.\nglamorous 与 fascinating 近义褒义替换\n【排除】A) 张冠李戴\n录音后面她说自己 it was very hard work，选项 It is challenging.（有挑战）蹭这层\"辛\n苦\"感，但那是她回顾奋斗时的自述，不是 many people 对这份工作的看法，答错了主体。\n借用原文：it was very hard work\nC) 与原文相反\nIt is tiresome.（令人厌烦）是负面评价，而很多人对这份工作的印象是 a glamorous job\n光鲜迷人，褒贬正好相反，录音也没人这么说。\nD) 原词陷阱\n录音里反复有 fashion（杂志名 Fashion magazine），选项 It is fashionable.（时髦的）\n借 fashion 与 fashionable 形近来诱选，但大众的看法是\"光鲜迷人\"而非\"时髦\"，且这话录\n音没说。\n借用原文：Fashion magazine", "points": ["听力·长对话", "态度观点题"]}, {"id": "2019-12-1-l-4", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 4, "stem": "What helped the woman to get her current position?", "options": ["A) Her persistence", "B) Her experience", "C) Her competence", "D) Her confidence"], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） Her persistence\n【定位】I had to be really persistent and it was very hard work.\n我必须非常坚持不懈，而且要付出非常艰辛的努力。\n【信号】被问者直接应答里的强调词。答案信号是她对\"如何克服困难\"这一问的直接应答开头：I had\nto be really persistent，really 加重语气强调 persistent（坚持不懈），是她明确点出的成\n功关键。\n【替换】I had to be really persistent ↔ Her persistence\n形容词 persistent 名词化为 persistence\n【排除】B) 原词陷阱\n录音末句确有 experience：all experiences make you a better journalist，选项 Her\nexperience 借这个原词诱选，但那句是说\"各种经历会让你成为更好的记者\"这一信念，并非\n她把\"经验\"当成拿到这份工作的直接原因，真正帮她的是\"坚持\"。\n借用原文：all experiences make you a better journalist\nC) 未提及\nHer competence（能力）与\"坚持\"都是褒义品质、意思相近极易混，但录音通篇没说是\"能\n力\"帮了她，她明确强调的是 persistent，competence 属编造。\nD) 未提及\nHer confidence（自信）也是褒义品质，听着像励志故事该有的词，但录音里她强调的是\nreally persistent\"坚持\"，从未说靠\"自信\"，属编造。", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2019-12-1-l-5", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 5, "stem": "What does the man think of the satire Frankie he recently watched?", "options": ["A) It is enjoyable.", "B) It is educational.", "C) It is divorced from real life.", "D) It is adapted from a drama."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （态度观点题） It is enjoyable.\n【定位】I'm watching a great satire called Frankie. I think you'd like it.\n我在看一部很棒的讽刺剧，叫《Frankie》。我想你会喜欢它的。\n【信号】评价形容词 great。男士一开口就用 a great satire 给出正面评价，great 这个评价形容词\n就是答案信号；后面 I think you'd like it 进一步印证他态度是正面的。\n【替换】a great satire ↔ It is enjoyable.\ngreat 同义于 enjoyable\n【排除】B) 过度推断\nIt is educational. 把好评具体化成『有教育意义』，但录音通篇只说它 great、funny，从\n没提它能教人东西，属过度推断。\nC) 原词陷阱\nIt is divorced from real life. 借了录音里 is divorced 的 divorced，但原文 divorced 指剧\n中人物『离婚』，根本不是说这剧『脱离现实』；且这是负评，与男士的正面态度相反。\n借用原文：is divorced\nD) 原词陷阱\nIt is adapted from a drama. 借了录音 a mixture of comedy and drama 里的 drama，\n但原文是说这剧『混合了喜剧与正剧元素』，不是说它『改编自某部戏剧』；而且本题问态\n度，这是来源描述，答非所问。\n借用原文：a mixture of comedy and drama", "points": ["听力·长对话", "态度观点题"]}, {"id": "2019-12-1-l-6", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 6, "stem": "What does the man say is special about the satire Frankie?", "options": ["A) All the roles are played by famous actors and actresses.", "B) It is based on the real-life experiences of some celebrities.", "C) Its plots and events reveal a lot about Frankie's actual life.", "D) It is written, directed, edited and produced by Frankie himself"], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） It is written, directed, edited and produced by Frankie himself\n【定位】In fact, the whole show is written, directed, edited and produced by him, and is very funny\nand has won many awards.\n事实上，整部剧都是由他一人编剧、导演、剪辑和制作的，非常好笑，还赢得了很多奖项。\n【信号】强调词 In fact。上一句女士说这是个 very original concept（很新颖的概念），男士用 In\nfact, the whole show is written, directed, edited and produced by him 接上，In fact\n是强调信号，引出这剧『特别』的核心——一人全包。\n【替换】written, directed, edited and produced by him ↔\nwritten, directed, edited and produced by Frankie himself\nhim 即 Frankie himself\n【排除】A) 与原文相反\nAll the roles are played by famous actors and actresses. 与原文相反：录音说 all his\nfriends and family are played by actors，只是普通 actors，且明确主角 he plays\nhimself，并非『所有角色都由名演员演』。\n借用原文：all his friends and family are played by actors\nB) 原词陷阱\nIt is based on the real-life experiences of some celebrities. 借了录音 a real-life series\nabout a real person 的 real-life，但原文随即被男士否定（It's fiction），且只涉及\nFrankie 一人，不是 some celebrities，主体与真假都对不上。\n借用原文：a real-life series about a real person\nC) 与原文相反\nIts plots and events reveal a lot about Frankie's actual life. 把原文借来的『真实生活』\n安在情节上，但录音说 the plots and events that take place are also invented——情节\n是虚构的，恰恰不反映真实生活，正好说反。\n借用原文：the plots and events that take place are also invented", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2019-12-1-l-7", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 7, "stem": "What does the woman say she is going to do with the satire Frankie?", "options": ["A) Go to the theater and enjoy it.", "B) Recommend it to her friends.", "C) Watch it with the man.", "D) Download and watch it."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） Download and watch it.\n【定位】That's cool. I will try to download it.\n真酷。我会试着把它下载下来看。\n【信号】意向句 I will。女士用 I will try to download it 直接表态自己的打算，I will 是表意向的信\n号词，紧跟的动作 download 就是答案。\n【替换】I will try to download it ↔ Download and watch it.\ndownload 即下载来看\n【排除】A) 未提及\nGo to the theater and enjoy it. 录音从未提到女士要去剧院看，她说的是下载，属无中生\n有。\nB) 张冠李戴\nRecommend it to her friends. 像是把男士那句 I think you'd like it（推荐给女士）的『推\n荐』动作挪到女士头上，但女士本人只说要下载，没说要推荐给朋友。\n借用原文：I think you'd like it\nC) 原词陷阱\nWatch it with the man. 里 watch 确是原文反复出现的词，但录音里女士只说自己下载来\n看，全程没有『和男士一起看』的安排，with the man 是凭空添加。\n借用原文：then you should check it out", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2019-12-1-l-8", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 8, "stem": "What does the woman say about the comedy The Big Bang Theory?", "options": ["A) It has drawn criticisms from scientists.", "B) It has been showing for over a decade.", "C) It is a ridiculous piece of satire.", "D) It is against common sense."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） It has been showing for over a decade.\n【定位】The show has been running for over ten years, and some of the actors are practically global\nsuperstars, now that they are such famous celebrities.\n这部剧已经播了十多年，其中一些演员实际上已成了全球超级巨星，如今他们都是大名鼎鼎\n的明星了。\n【信号】并列陈述里的事实。女士在 some of the actors are practically global superstars, now\nthat they are such famous celebrities 里给出一条客观事实——部分演员已成名人；这种\n可核对的事实陈述就是细节题的答案落点。\n【替换】some of the actors are practically global superstars, now that they are such famous celebrities ↔\nIt is based on the real-life experiences of some celebrities.\n演员成名人对应 celebrities\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\nIt has drawn criticisms from scientists. 借了录音 four male scientists 的 scientists，但\n原文只是说剧里有四个科学家角色，从没说这剧『招来科学家批评』，批评是无中生有。\n借用原文：four male scientists\nC) 张冠李戴\nIt is a ridiculous piece of satire. 里 satire 是男士形容《Frankie》的词（a great\nsatire），并非女士对《The Big Bang Theory》的评价；况且女士说它 very funny、a\nhuge hit，从无 ridiculous 之贬。\n借用原文：a great satire\nD) 与原文相反\nIt is against common sense. 盗用录音 the lady's social skills and common sense 的\ncommon sense，但原文是说那位女士『有 common sense（常识）』，根本不是说这部\n剧『违背常识』，意思正好相反。\n借用原文：the lady's social skills and common sense", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2019-12-1-l-9", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 9, "stem": "What does the speaker say about players of contact sports?", "options": ["A) They are likely to get injured when moving too fast.", "B) They believe in team spirit for good performance.", "C) They need to keep moving to avoid getting hurt.", "D) They have to learn how to avoid body contact."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） They need to keep moving to avoid getting hurt.\n【定位】Those who play contact sports know that the player who usually gets hurt the most is the one\nwho is standing still.\n从事身体接触类运动的人都知道，通常受伤最重的，恰恰是那个站着不动的人。\n【信号】出现题干同款话题词 contact sports，其后即答案。录音里 Those who play contact\nsports 与题干 contact sports 完全同词，是顺听定位的信号灯；说话人讲到这个话题词后\n紧接的判断 the player who usually gets hurt the most is the one who is standing still\n就是答案句。\n【替换】the player who usually gets hurt the most is the one who is standing still ↔\nThey need to keep moving to avoid getting hurt.\n'站着不动的人最受伤'反过来即'要保持移动才不受伤'\n【排除】A) 与原文相反\nThey are likely to get injured when moving too fast. 把受伤怪到'移动太快'头上，而原\n文说受伤最重的是 the one who is standing still——是'不动'受伤，不是'动太快'受伤，方\n向恰好反了。\n借用原文：the player who usually gets hurt the most is the one who is standing still\nB) 未提及\nThey believe in team spirit for good performance. 讲'团队精神'，但录音谈接触类运动\n只说了'不动的人最易受伤'，全程没出现 team spirit 或团队配合的内容，属凭'运动→团\n队'常识硬填。\nD) 原词陷阱\nThey have to learn how to avoid body contact. 里的 body contact 借了原文 Those\nwho play contact sports 的 contact 一词，但原文是说'接触类运动里不动的人最受伤'，\n从没说要去'避免身体接触'，是听到 contact 就想选的原词陷阱。\n借用原文：Those who play contact sports", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2019-12-1-l-10", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 10, "stem": "What do longevity statistics reveal about the average person?", "options": ["A) They do not have many years to live after retirement.", "B) They tend to live a longer life with early retirement.", "C) They do not start enjoying life until full retirement.", "D) They keep themselves busy even after retirement."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） They do not have many years to live after retirement.\n【定位】Longevity statistics reveal that the average person doesn't last very long after retirement.\n寿命统计数据显示，普通人在退休之后活不了多久。\n【信号】题干锚词 statistics reveal 在原文原样出现，紧跟即答案。录音 Longevity statistics\nreveal 与题干用词完全一致，是定位信号；其后宾语从句 the average person doesn't\nlast very long after retirement 就是统计数据揭示的内容，即答案句。\n【替换】the average person doesn't last very long after retirement ↔\nThey do not have many years to live after retirement.\n'活不了多久'＝'没有多少年可活'\n【排除】B) 与原文相反\nThey tend to live a longer life with early retirement. 说早退休反而更长寿，而原文是\nthe average person doesn't last very long after retirement——退休后活不久，且后文\n紧接 The moral here is don't retire，劝人别退休；选项把原文意思反了过来。\n借用原文：the average person doesn't last very long after retirement\nC) 原词陷阱\nThey do not start enjoying life until full retirement. 借了原文反复出现的 retirement，\n造出'要等完全退休才开始享受生活'，但原文谈退休只说'活不久、别退休'，根本没谈'何时开\n始享受生活'，是借原词的答非所问。\n借用原文：the average person doesn't last very long after retirement\nD) 张冠李戴\nThey keep themselves busy even after retirement. 与全篇 keep moving、stay\ninvolved 主题字面相符，借了主旨词诱选；但本题问的是'统计数据揭示普通人怎样'，答案\n是'退休后活不久'，而非描述他们'退休后仍忙碌'，张冠李戴。\n借用原文：that is how he got to be 94, by staying involved", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2019-12-1-l-11", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 11, "stem": "What bonus does the \"keep moving\" principle bring us according to the speaker?", "options": ["A) It prevents us from worrying.", "B) It slows down our aging process.", "C) It enables us to accomplish more in life.", "D) It provides us with more chances to learn."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） It prevents us from worrying.\n【定位】A bonus with the \"keep moving\" principle is that while we keep moving, we don't have a\nchance to worry.\n\"保持移动\"原则带来的一个额外好处是：当我们不停活动时，就没有机会去忧虑。\n【信号】题干同款词 a bonus 领起，正是好处提示句。录音 A bonus with the \"keep moving\"\nprinciple is that 与题干 bonus 完全同词，是定位信号；其后 while we keep moving, we\ndon't have a chance to worry 即点出好处，为答案句。\n【替换】we don't have a chance to worry ↔ It prevents us from worrying.\n'没机会去忧虑'＝'防止我们忧虑'\n【排除】B) 未提及\nIt slows down our aging process. 在养生话题里很顺耳，但本题答案句说的好处是 we\ndon't have a chance to worry，全文也没说 keep moving 能 slow down aging，是用常\n识硬填的未提及项。\nC) 答非所问\nIt enables us to accomplish more in life. 借了前文名人成就(写作、获奖、作画到八九十\n岁)的语境联想，但那是第 3 段讲长寿的例子，本题问的是第 4 段的 bonus，而 bonus 明\n确是'没机会担忧'，并非'让人成就更多'，答非所问。\n借用原文：Benjamin Franklin produced some of his best writings at the age of 84\nD) 原词陷阱\nIt provides us with more chances to learn. 借了第 2 段 keep moving, extending and\nlearning 里的 learning，但那是讲 keep moving 的内涵、不是第 4 段问的 bonus；本题\nbonus 是'没空担忧'，与'更多学习机会'无关，是听到 learn 就想选的原词陷阱。\n借用原文：the essential message is \"keep moving, extending and learning.\"", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2019-12-1-l-12", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 12, "stem": "What does the passage say about people's mind?", "options": ["A) It tends to dwell upon their joyous experiences.", "B) It wanders for almost half of their waking time.", "C) It has trouble concentrating after a brain injury.", "D) It tends to be affected by their negative feelings."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） It wanders for almost half of their waking time.\n【定位】By one estimate, people daydream through nearly half of their waking hours.\n据某项估计，人们在将近一半的清醒时间里都在走神（做白日梦）。\n【信号】数字估计句即落点。本句以 By one estimate（据估计）领出一个量化结论 nearly half of\ntheir waking hours，篇章里凡跟着 \"据估计/研究发现\" 的量化句，往往就是承载答案的总\n述句，是听力定位的强信号。\n【替换】daydream through nearly half of their waking hours ↔\nwanders for almost half of their waking time\ndaydream=wanders、hours≈time 同义改写\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\nA 说大脑 dwell upon their joyous experiences（沉湎于愉快经历），看似呼应原文 more\nlikely to wander toward pleasant topics，但那是讲走神时偏向愉快话题、并非本题问的\n\"大脑总体特点\"，属答非所问，且 \"沉湎/dwell upon\" 原文也未说。\n借用原文：They were more likely to wander toward pleasant topics than unpleasant ones.\nC) 未提及\nC 说大脑 trouble concentrating after a brain injury（脑损伤后难以集中），原文全篇讲\n的是走神（mind wandering）与白日梦，从未提到 brain injury（脑损伤）这回事，属凭\n空捏造。\nD) 原词陷阱\nD 说大脑 affected by their negative feelings（受负面情绪影响），借用了原文 wander\nmore when they felt upset（心烦时更易走神）里的 upset，但原文是说 \"走神频率\" 受情\n绪影响、且偏向愉快话题，并非笼统说大脑被负面情绪左右，是把局部信息夸大、错置到本\n题。\n借用原文：Respondents' minds tended to wander more when they felt upset rather than happy.", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2019-12-1-l-13", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 13, "stem": "For what purpose did the researchers develop the new app?", "options": ["A) To find how happiness relates to daydreaming.", "B) To observe how one's mind affects one's behavior.", "C) To see why daydreaming impacts what one is doing.", "D) To study the relation between health and daydreaming."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （目的题） To find how happiness relates to daydreaming.\n【定位】For one experiment, researchers developed an app to analyze the relationship between\ndaydreaming and happiness.\n在一项实验中，研究人员开发了一款应用程序，用来分析白日梦（走神）与幸福之间的\n关系。\n【信号】to do 即目的落点。句中 developed an app to analyze the relationship between\ndaydreaming and happiness 用 to analyze 直接交代开发 app 的目的，目的状语 to do\n后面跟的就是答案，是目的题最硬的定位信号。\n【替换】to analyze the relationship between daydreaming and happiness ↔\nTo find how happiness relates to daydreaming\nanalyze the relationship=find how…relates 同义\n【排除】B) 原词陷阱\nB 说目的是 To observe how one's mind affects one's behavior（观察心智如何影响行\n为），借用了原文反复出现的 mind 一词，但原文 app 是分析 \"走神与幸福\" 的关系，并非\n\"心智与行为\" 的关系，宾语被偷换。\n借用原文：to analyze the relationship between daydreaming and happiness\nC) 原词陷阱\nC 说目的是 To see why daydreaming impacts what one is doing（看走神为何影响手头\n的事），其中 \"走神影响手头的事\" 呼应了原文 think about something other than the\ntask one is doing，但那是前文对走神现象的定义，不是开发 app 的目的，属答非所问。\n借用原文：the tendency to think about something other than the task one is doing\nD) 偷换概念\nD 说目的是研究 the relation between health and daydreaming（健康与走神的关系），\n把原文的 happiness（幸福）偷换成读音相近的 health（健康），其余几乎照搬，是最贴\n近正确项、最易听岔的陷阱。\n借用原文：the relationship between daydreaming and happiness", "points": ["听力·篇章", "目的题"]}, {"id": "2019-12-1-l-14", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 14, "stem": "How does daydreaming benefit people according to some researchers?", "options": ["A) It helps them make good decisions.", "B) It helps them tap their potentials.", "C) It contributes to their creativity.", "D) It contributes to clear thinking"], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） It contributes to their creativity.\n【定位】Yet other research suggests that daydreaming has benefits. Researchers have found that it\ngives us a chance to think about our goals and it also seems to increase creativity.\n然而另一些研究表明，走神（白日梦）也有好处。研究人员发现，它让我们有机会思考自己\n的目标，似乎还能提升创造力。\n【信号】转折Yet后才是好处。本段以 Yet 转折，把前文走神的坏处（开车走神出事故）一转，转折\n后才是本题要的好处；篇章里 yet / but / however 之后的内容常是真正落点，听到转折词\n要立刻提起注意。\n【替换】it also seems to increase creativity ↔ It contributes to their creativity\nincrease=contributes to 同义\n【排除】A) 过度推断\nA 说走神帮人 make good decisions（做好决定），看似贴近原文 think about our goals\n（思考目标），但 \"思考目标\" 不等于 \"做好决定\"，原文只说给机会想目标，没说由此能做\n好决策，属过度推断。\n借用原文：it gives us a chance to think about our goals\nB) 未提及\nB 说走神帮人 tap their potentials（发掘潜能），原文讲到的好处只有 \"思考目标\" 和 \"提\n升创造力\"，从未提及 potential（潜能），属凭空添加。\nD) 未提及\nD 说走神带来 clear thinking（清晰的思考），原文列的好处是 think about goals 与\nincrease creativity，并无 \"使思考更清晰\" 之说，属无中生有的近义干扰。", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2019-12-1-l-15", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 15, "stem": "What was the finding of the experiment with 145 undergraduates?", "options": ["A) Subjects with clear goals in mind outperformed those without clear goals.", "B) The difference in performance between the two groups was insignificant.", "C) Non-daydreamers were more focused on their tasks than daydreamers.", "D) Daydreamers did better than non-daydreamers in task performance."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） Daydreamers did better than non-daydreamers in task performance.\n【定位】When all the participants proceeded to the second pair of tasks, the daydreamers performed\n40 percent better than the others.\n当所有受试者进行第二组任务时，走神（做白日梦）的那一组比其他人表现好了百分之\n四十。\n【信号】比较+百分比即结论。句中 performed 40 percent better than the others 是带百分比的\n比较句，出现在整段实验描述的最后，正是实验结论；实验题里 \"X% better\nthan\"\"outperformed\" 这类末尾比较句就是答案信号。\n【替换】the daydreamers performed 40 percent better than the others ↔\nDaydreamers did better than non-daydreamers in task performance\nperformed better=did better、the others=non-daydreamers\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\nA 说 Subjects with clear goals in mind outperformed those without clear goals（有明\n确目标者胜过无目标者），把分组标准说成 \"有无清晰目标\"，但本实验分组是 \"被安排走神\nvs 没被安排\"，且 goals 一词来自第4段前文 think about our goals 而非本实验，属张冠李\n戴的捏造。\n借用原文：it gives us a chance to think about our goals\nB) 与原文相反\nB 说两组 The difference in performance between the two groups was insignificant\n（差异不显著），而原文明说差了 40 percent better——这是巨大差异，B 与原文结论完\n全相反。\n借用原文：the daydreamers performed 40 percent better than the others\nC) 与原文相反\nC 说 Non-daydreamers were more focused on their tasks than daydreamers（非走神\n者比走神者更专注于任务），把胜出方向反过来了——原文是 daydreamers 表现更好，且\n原文讲的是 \"表现\" 而非 \"专注度\"，方向和维度都错。\n借用原文：the daydreamers performed 40 percent better than the others", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2019-12-1-l-16", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 16, "stem": "What does the speaker say about the Viking wooden churches?", "options": ["A) They are the oldest buildings in Europe.", "B) They are part of the Christian tradition.", "C) They are renovated to attract tourists.", "D) They are in worsening condition."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） They are in worsening condition.\n【定位】But now, with only 30 wooden churches remaining and their condition deteriorating, experts\nare working to preserve the structures for future generations.\n但如今，仅存 30 座木教堂、且它们的状况正在恶化，专家们正努力为后代保护这些建筑。\n【信号】转折 But now 后才是真意。前半句还在夸它们 the rarest historic buildings，一个 But\nnow 把话锋转向现状，转折之后的 their condition deteriorating 才是说话人要落的点\n——听到 but now 就该把耳朵竖起来。\n【替换】their condition deteriorating ↔ in worsening condition\ndeteriorating = worsening 同义改写\n【排除】A) 偷换概念\n原文夸它们是 the rarest historic buildings in western Europe（最稀有），A 偷偷把\nrarest 换成 oldest（最古老）——稀有不等于最古老，听到“最高级 + 西欧”就想选就中套\n了。\n借用原文：the rarest historic buildings in western Europe\nB) 过度推断\n原文只说它们是 wooden churches（木教堂），借“教堂”让你脑补“基督教传统”，但全篇真\n正定调的是 Scandinavia's Viking past（维京渊源），从没把它们说成基督教传统的一部\n分，属过度推断。\n借用原文：Scandinavia's Viking past\nC) 未提及\n原文说专家在 working to preserve the structures for future generations（为后代保\n护），C 却说成 renovated to attract tourists（为引客而翻新）——保护目的被换成招揽\n游客，原文根本没提 tourists，属无中生有。", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2019-12-1-l-17", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 17, "stem": "What is special about most of the Viking wooden churches?", "options": ["A) They have a history of 14 centuries.", "B) They are 40 metres tall on average.", "C) They are without foundations.", "D) They consist of several storeys."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） They are without foundations.\n【定位】Most consist of timber frames that rest on stone blocks. This means that they have\nno foundations.\n大多数教堂由架在石块上的木构架组成。这意味着它们没有地基。\n【信号】Most + This means 推出特征。题干问的是 most（大多数），原文正以 Most consist of\n开头，紧接一句 This means that they have no foundations 把特征点破——This means\n后面的那句结论就是说话人要你抓的特别之处。\n【替换】they have no foundations ↔ without foundations\nhave no = without 否定同义\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\n原文说它们 Dating from as far back as the 12th century（最早可追到 12 世纪），A 把\n“12 世纪”歪算成“14 个世纪的历史”——12th century 是年代，14 centuries 是时长，听到\n数字就抓极易上当。\n借用原文：Dating from as far back as the 12th century\nB) 原词陷阱\n原文说有些大的 soar up to 40 metres（最高可达 40 米），B 却说成 40 metres tall on\naverage（平均高 40 米）——up to（至多）被偷换成 on average（平均），且这只描述\n“大的那些”不是 most。\n借用原文：They soar up to 40 metres into the cold air\nD) 与原文相反\n原文明说它们 normally feature only a single storey（通常只有单层），D 却说 several\nstoreys（好几层）——只是多了几个不同的屋顶层 roof levels，被偷换成“好几层楼”，与\n原文相反。\n借用原文：they normally feature only a single storey but numerous different roof levels", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2019-12-1-l-18", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 18, "stem": "Why were the Viking churches constructed from wood?", "options": ["A) Wood was harmonious with nature.", "B) Wooden buildings kept the cold out.", "C) Timber was abundant in Scandinavia.", "D) The Vikings liked wooden structures."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） Timber was abundant in Scandinavia.\n【定位】The reason for constructing the buildings from wood is probably that ideally proportioned\nstraight and slender timber was available in large quantities in Scandinavia's vast pine forests.\n用木材建造这些建筑的原因，很可能是斯堪的纳维亚广袤的松林里盛产比例匀称、笔直纤细\n的木材。\n【信号】The reason…is 直接点原因。原文用 The reason for constructing the buildings from\nwood is probably that 把原因明明白白引出来，后半句 available in large quantities 就\n是真因——“The reason…is”是最直白的因果信号，听到就锁。\n【替换】timber was available in large quantities in Scandinavia's vast pine forests ↔\nTimber was abundant in Scandinavia.\navailable in large quantities = abundant 概括\n【排除】A) 未提及\n“木头与自然和谐”听着很顺，但原文给的原因是木材量大又便宜（cheaper to use than the\nstone），从没说过 harmonious with nature，属无中生有。\nB) 未提及\n原文只提到 the cold air（冷空气）描述教堂高耸的环境，B 借这个“冷”字脑补成“木建筑御\n寒”，但用木头的原因和保暖毫无关系，属未提及。\n借用原文：They soar up to 40 metres into the cold air\nD) 张冠李戴\n原文里 Vikings 出现在“造船传统”和“雕刻技艺源自维京”的语境（partly established by the\nVikings），D 却把它说成“维京人喜欢木结构”——用木的真因是“木材多又便宜”，把原因安\n到“维京偏好”头上，是张冠李戴。\n借用原文：partly established by the Vikings", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2019-12-1-l-19", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 19, "stem": "What are the three interesting studies about?", "options": ["A) Similarities between human babies and baby animals.", "B) Cognitive features of different newly born mammals.", "C) Adults' influence on children.", "D) Abilities of human babies."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （主旨题） Abilities of human babies.\n【定位】But in today's lecture, we'll look at three very interesting studies that hint at the surprising\nabilities of human babies.\n但在今天的讲座里，我们将看三项非常有趣的研究，它们暗示了人类婴儿令人惊讶的能力。\n【信号】转折 But 后的点题句。前一句先说上周讲的是哺乳动物、人类婴儿\"physically less\ndeveloped\"，紧接着用 But in today's lecture 转折，转折之后才是今天的真正主题；篇章\n独白里开头的 But / Today 之后那句通常就是全篇点题句。\n【替换】the surprising abilities of human babies ↔ Abilities of human babies\n去掉修饰的同义概括\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\n录音开头说上周讨论过 the newly born offspring of several mammals 并把人类婴儿与其\n他哺乳动物作比，A 把\"人类婴儿与动物幼崽的相似\"当成今天主题，实际那是上周内容、\n且\"相似\"也非录音重点，是用旧话题骗\"听到 babies 和 animals 就选\"。\n借用原文：we discussed the characteristics of the newly born offspring of several mammals\nB) 原词陷阱\n录音确有 other mammals of the same age 一语，B 借此把主题说成\"不同新生哺乳动物\n的认知特征\"，但今天三项研究全讲人类婴儿、哺乳动物只是引子，这是把范围错放到了哺\n乳动物身上。\n借用原文：human infants are less developed physically than other mammals of the same age\nC) 未提及\nC 说三项研究是关于\"成人对孩子的影响\"，但录音三项研究分别讲婴儿的记忆、辨乐、情\n绪，全程没有\"成人影响孩子\"这一主题（第四项里成人只是观察者），属凭空捏造。", "points": ["听力·讲座", "主旨题"]}, {"id": "2019-12-1-l-20", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 20, "stem": "What does the second study find about nine-month-old babies?", "options": ["A) They can distinguish a happy tune from a sad one.", "B) They love happy melodies more than sad ones.", "C) They fall asleep easily while listening to music.", "D) They are already sensitive to beats and rhythms."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） They can distinguish a happy tune from a sad one.\n【定位】This suggests that babies of that age can tell the difference between a happy melody and a\nsad tune.\n这表明那个月龄的婴儿能够分辨欢快的旋律和悲伤的曲调。\n【信号】结论句 This suggests。实验描述之后用 This suggests that 引出研究结论，研究发现题\n的答案几乎都落在 suggests / shows / found 这类结论句上，而不在前面的实验过程描\n述里。\n【替换】can tell the difference between a happy melody and a sad tune ↔\ndistinguish a happy tune from a sad one\ntell the difference=distinguish\n【排除】B) 原词陷阱\n录音通篇讲的是婴儿\"能分辨\"快乐与悲伤的曲子，从未说他们\"更喜欢快乐的\"；B 借用了\nhappy / sad 这组录音真实出现的词，却把\"能区分\"偷换成\"更偏爱欢快的\"，是 A 的对立干\n扰。\n借用原文：either from sad to happy or the other way around\nC) 原词陷阱\n录音里婴儿是\"got bored and averted their gaze\"（厌倦后移开视线），C 却说他们\"听音\n乐容易睡着\"；它借 music 这个真实话题词，编造了录音从未提到的\"睡着\"反应。\n借用原文：They waited until the babies got bored and averted their gaze from the face\nD) 未提及\nD 说婴儿\"对节拍和韵律敏感\"，但本段只讲\"能分辨快乐与悲伤的曲调\"，从未提及 beats\nand rhythms；这是引入录音没有的新概念。", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2019-12-1-l-21", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 21, "stem": "What is the 1980 study about?", "options": ["A) Infants' facial expressions.", "B) Babies' emotions.", "C) Babies' interaction with adult.", "D) Infants' behaviors."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （主旨题） Babies' emotions.\n【定位】In fact, it's one of the most famous pieces of research about infant emotion ever published.\n事实上，它是有史以来发表过的关于婴儿情绪的最著名研究之一。\n【信号】定性句 research about。录音用 research about infant emotion 直接给这项研究下定\n义，about 后面的词就是研究主题；定性句优先于后文列举的表情、互动等具体环节。\n【替换】research about infant emotion ↔ Babies' emotions\ninfant=baby、emotion同词\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\n录音确实提到婴儿 made various facial expressions，A 借此说研究\"关于面部表情\"；但表\n情只是观察手段——成人通过看表情来判断情绪，研究真正的主题是\"情绪\"。这是最像对的\n原词陷阱。\n借用原文：the babies made various facial expressions\nC) 原词陷阱\n录音说视频里的事件\"including playful interactions\"，C 借 interaction 说研究\"关于婴儿\n与成人的互动\"；但互动只是触发婴儿表情的情境之一，并非研究主题，且 C 还把它说成\"与\n成人的互动\"，偏离了研究核心。\n借用原文：real-life events including playful interactions and painful ones\nD) 偷换概念\nD 用\"婴儿的行为\"这一最宽泛的词概括研究，但录音明确定性为关于\"情绪\"的研究，\"行\n为\"既未被录音用来定义这项研究、范围也过大，是以宽代窄的干扰。\n借用原文：research about infant emotion", "points": ["听力·讲座", "主旨题"]}, {"id": "2019-12-1-l-22", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 22, "stem": "What does the speaker say about being over collaborative?", "options": ["A) It may harm the culture of today's workplace.", "B) It may hinder individual career advancement.", "C) It may result in unwillingness to take risks.", "D) It may put too much pressure on team members."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） It may hinder individual career advancement.\n【定位】However, placing too much emphasis on being a good team player can negatively affect your\ncareer growth.\n然而，过分强调做一个好的团队成员，会对你的职业成长产生负面影响。\n【信号】转折词 However 后才是真意。前一句还在夸团队精神是必备技能，However, placing too\nmuch emphasis on being a good team player can negatively affect your career\ngrowth. 用 However 一转，才点出过度协作的核心危害——这句转折后的话就是答案句。\n【替换】negatively affect your career growth ↔ hinder individual career advancement\nnegatively affect=hinder，career growth=career advancement\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\n录音第一段确实念到 our modern workplace，A 项 It may harm the culture of today's\nworkplace. 借这个 workplace 制造\"听到就想选\"的错觉，但原文从未说过过度协作会损\n害\"职场文化\"，危害对象是\"你的职业成长\"，不是文化。\n借用原文：our modern workplace\nC) 未提及\nIt may result in unwillingness to take risks. 说的\"导致不愿冒险\"在录音里找不到任何对应\n表述，是凭\"过度协作好像会让人保守\"的常识硬推出来的。\nD) 原词陷阱\nIt may put too much pressure on team members. 套着\"团队成员\"这个全篇高频语境，\n但原文讲的危害是\"拖累你自己的发展\"，从没说过\"给团队成员太大压力\"，方向答非所问。\n借用原文：being a good team player", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2019-12-1-l-23", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 23, "stem": "What does the speaker say about people engaged in collaborative projects?", "options": ["A) They can hardly give expression to their original views.", "B) They can become less motivated to do projects of their own.", "C) They may find it hard to get their contributions recognized.", "D) They may eventually lose their confidence and creativity"], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） They may find it hard to get their contributions recognized.\n【定位】Collaboration certainly makes your individual competencies and contributions more difficult\nfor outsiders to identify. Collaborative projects mean you're sharing the spotlight with others.\nOutsiders may then find it difficult to determine your contributions and strengths.\n协作确实会让外人更难看清你个人的能力与贡献。协作项目意味着你要和别人分享聚光灯。\n于是外人可能很难判断出你的贡献和长处。\n【信号】围绕 contributions 反复强调的核心句。这一段三句都在说同一件事——more difficult\nfor outsiders to identify、Outsiders may then find it difficult to determine your\ncontributions and strengths.，反复强调\"贡献被外人难以识别\"，这种重复正是答案\n落点。\n【替换】Outsiders may then find it difficult to determine your contributions and strengths ↔\nThey may find it hard to get their contributions recognized\noutsiders determine your contributions=get contributions recognized\n【排除】A) 张冠李戴\nThey can hardly give expression to their original views. 像第二段\"too silent about\ntheir own point of view\"的影子，但那段讲的是\"过度迁就者\"，不是本题问的\"做协作项目\n的人\"，且本题答案句明确在讲\"贡献难被识别\"，与\"表达观点\"不是一回事。\n借用原文：too silent about their own point of view\nB) 未提及\nThey can become less motivated to do projects of their own. 把\"积极性下降、不愿做\n个人项目\"当后果，但录音第三段只讲\"贡献难被外人看清\"，从没说协作会让人\"丧失做个人\n项目的动力\"，属凭推测添加。\nD) 过度推断\nThey may eventually lose their confidence and creativity 里 confidence 与第二段\nunderconfident 沾边，但那是讲过度迁就者的\"品牌形象\"，本题答案句根本没提\nconfidence 或 creativity，且 eventually lose 把后果夸到\"丧失\"，是过度推断。\n借用原文：underconfident", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2019-12-1-l-24", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 24, "stem": "How do people benefit from working in a team?", "options": ["A) They can enlarge their professional circle.", "B) They can get chances to engage in research.", "C) They can make the best use of their expertise.", "D) They can complete the project more easily."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） They can enlarge their professional circle.\n【定位】You then have more opportunities to expand your professional network than if you\nworked alone.\n于是相比单干，你会有更多机会去扩展自己的职业人脉网络。\n【信号】benefits 后列举的具体好处句。本段以 Working in a team can have huge benefits 开头\n点出\"团队有大好处\"，随后 more opportunities to expand your professional network\n就是它列出的那条具体好处——益处总起句之后紧跟的具体项即答案。\n【替换】expand your professional network ↔ enlarge their professional circle\nexpand=enlarge，network=circle\n【排除】B) 未提及\nThey can get chances to engage in research. 里的 research 在整篇录音里从未出现，是\n凭空添加的好处。\nC) 原词陷阱\nexpertise 确在第六段念过（Collaborate with people who have complementary\nexpertise），C 项 They can make the best use of their expertise. 借此诱选，但那段讲\n的是\"如何挑选合作对象\"，且原文是\"找互补专长的人\"，不是\"用好自己的专长\"，答非所\n问。\n借用原文：complementary expertise\nD) 未提及\nThey can complete the project more easily. 听上去\"人多好办事\"很顺，但录音讲团队好\n处时只提\"重复的成功、被认可、扩展人脉\"，从没说\"项目能更轻松完成\"，是常识脑补。", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2019-12-1-l-25", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 25, "stem": "Why is it undesirable to spend too much time trying to reach consensus?", "options": ["A) It may cause lots of arguments in a team.", "B) It may prevent making a timely decision.", "C) It may give rise to a lot of unnecessary expenses.", "D) It may deprive a team of business opportunities."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） It may prevent making a timely decision.\n【定位】When we're too collaborative, we want everyone to agree with a decision before we proceed.\nThis can create unnecessary delays as you hold meetings trying to achieve a consensus.\n当我们过度协作时，我们想在推进前让每个人都同意某个决定。这会造成不必要的拖延，因\n为你要不停开会去达成共识。\n【信号】因果句 This can create…+ delays 是后果落点。This can create unnecessary delays as\nyou hold meetings trying to achieve a consensus. 用 This can create…直接给出\"耗时\n求共识\"的后果——不必要的拖延，这句因果就是答案句。\n【替换】This can create unnecessary delays ↔ It may prevent making a timely decision\ncreate delays=prevent a timely decision\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\n本段反复念 achieve a consensus，A 项 It may cause lots of arguments in a team.\n借\"共识/团队\"语境诱选，但原文讲的后果是\"拖延\"不是\"争论\"——求共识恰恰是为了避免分\n歧，说\"引发大量争论\"反而拧了。\n借用原文：achieve a consensus\nC) 偷换概念\nIt may give rise to a lot of unnecessary expenses. 顺着 you hold meetings 的开会画面\n联想到\"花钱\"，但原文 unnecessary 修饰的是 delays（拖延）不是 expenses（开销），\n是把\"不必要的拖延\"偷换成了\"不必要的花费\"。\n借用原文：unnecessary delays\nD) 过度推断\nIt may deprive a team of business opportunities. 说的\"错失商业机会\"在末段找不到对\n应，是从\"拖延→可能误事\"再往前推了一步的脑补，原文止于\"拖延\"。", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2019-12-1-c-26", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 26, "stem": "", "options": ["A) assessed", "B) assigned", "C) consequences", "D) conspicuously", "E) deficits", "F) designated", "G) detrimental", "H) digestion", "I) excelling", "J) indulging", "K) loopholes", "L) rapidly", "M) redundant", "N) regularly", "O) similar"], "answer": "G", "analysis": "【答案】G) detrimental\n形容词\n【词性槽】the ___ effects 名词前缺定语\n【依据】与\"unhealthy/insufficient\"语义同向，\"有害的影响\"\n空的左右语境 an unhealthy diet and insufficient exercise 都是负面要素，effects 应承接\n其\"负面影响\"基调；detrimental（有害的）正好与\"unhealthy/insufficient\"在感情色彩上\n一致，构成 detrimental effects（有害影响）这一医学/科普英语高频搭配。\n【竞争词】M) redundant 义为\"多余的/被裁的\"，方向不对\nredundant effects（多余的影响）不是地道说法，也与全文\"危害\"主线不相干；这个词在英语里\n更多用于职位被裁或信息冗余。\nO) similar 需要 to 引出对照对象，且语义空洞\nsimilar 通常要跟 similar to sth，单独说 the similar effects 既缺对照、又只是\"相似的\"——填\n进去等于没说，留给后面真正需要\"相似\"语义的空（如 32 题）更合适。", "points": ["选词填空", "形容词"]}, {"id": "2019-12-1-c-27", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 27, "stem": "", "options": ["A) assessed", "B) assigned", "C) consequences", "D) conspicuously", "E) deficits", "F) designated", "G) detrimental", "H) digestion", "I) excelling", "J) indulging", "K) loopholes", "L) rapidly", "M) redundant", "N) regularly", "O) similar"], "answer": "J", "analysis": "【答案】J) indulging\n动词\n【词性槽】that ___ in a ... diet 从句主语后缺谓语动词\n【依据】indulge in 沉迷于… 与负面饮食贴合\nindulge in 是固定搭配，意为\"沉溺/纵情于\"，常用于不健康行为；indulging in a high-fat\nand high-sugar diet = \"沉溺于高脂高糖饮食\"，与下文 may have negative effects on\nyour brain 的负面定性完全吻合。\n【竞争词】I) excelling 义为\"擅长\"，与高脂高糖饮食感情色彩相悖\nexcel in 表示\"在某方面出类拔萃\"，是正面感情色彩；\"excelling in a high-fat and high-sugar\ndiet\"（在高脂高糖饮食上出类拔萃）既不合英语习惯，又与全文负面论调完全对立。", "points": ["选词填空", "动词"]}, {"id": "2019-12-1-c-28", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 28, "stem": "", "options": ["A) assessed", "B) assigned", "C) consequences", "D) conspicuously", "E) deficits", "F) designated", "G) detrimental", "H) digestion", "I) excelling", "J) indulging", "K) loopholes", "L) rapidly", "M) redundant", "N) regularly", "O) similar"], "answer": "E", "analysis": "【答案】E) deficits\n名词\n【词性槽】causing learning and memory ___ 动词宾语缺名词\n【依据】learning and memory deficits 是学术固定说法\ndeficits 在认知科学英语里专指\"功能性缺损\"，learning and memory deficits 是医学/心\n理学论文的常见短语，意为\"学习与记忆缺陷\"，恰好对应 causing 后\"造成…\"的负面结果。\n【竞争词】H) digestion 不可数且偏离主题\ndigestion 是\"消化\"，与 learning and memory 组合既不自然也不指向认知损伤。\nK) loopholes 语义场完全错位\nloopholes（漏洞）一般用在制度/规则语境，learning and memory loopholes 不是英语固定\n表达，逻辑也不通。", "points": ["选词填空", "名词"]}, {"id": "2019-12-1-c-29", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 29, "stem": "", "options": ["A) assessed", "B) assigned", "C) consequences", "D) conspicuously", "E) deficits", "F) designated", "G) detrimental", "H) digestion", "I) excelling", "J) indulging", "K) loopholes", "L) rapidly", "M) redundant", "N) regularly", "O) similar"], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A) assessed\n动词\n【词性槽】as ___ by a range of ... tests 后置定语缺过去分词\n【依据】\"由测试评估\"——assess by tests 学术固定搭配\nassess（评估）正是科研写作里描述\"通过某种测试衡量\"的标准动词：as assessed by a\nrange of learning and memory tests = \"正如由一系列学习与记忆测试所评估的那样\"，\n与前面 impairments in cognitive functioning 自然衔接。\n【竞争词】B) assigned 义为\"分配/指派\"，与 by tests 搭配不通\nassign 多用于\"把人/任务分配给…\"，不用于\"由测试分配\"；as assigned by tests 在语义上不\n成立。\nF) designated 义为\"指定\"，与\"由测试…\"逻辑不接\ndesignate 一般是\"将某物指定为某身份/某用途\"，与\"由测试…\"不构成自然搭配；语义上也无法\n说\"被测试指定\"。", "points": ["选词填空", "动词"]}, {"id": "2019-12-1-c-30", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 30, "stem": "", "options": ["A) assessed", "B) assigned", "C) consequences", "D) conspicuously", "E) deficits", "F) designated", "G) detrimental", "H) digestion", "I) excelling", "J) indulging", "K) loopholes", "L) rapidly", "M) redundant", "N) regularly", "O) similar"], "answer": "L", "analysis": "【答案】L) rapidly\n副词\n【词性槽】can emerge ___ 动词后缺方式副词\n【依据】破折号后\"几周乃至几天\"=快\n破折号后 within weeks or even days 直接定义了\"多快\"，即\"很快\"；rapidly（迅速地）正\n是这一速度感的最直接表达。", "points": ["选词填空", "副词"]}, {"id": "2019-12-1-c-31", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 31, "stem": "", "options": ["A) assessed", "B) assigned", "C) consequences", "D) conspicuously", "E) deficits", "F) designated", "G) detrimental", "H) digestion", "I) excelling", "J) indulging", "K) loopholes", "L) rapidly", "M) redundant", "N) regularly", "O) similar"], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B) assigned\n动词\n【词性槽】adults ___ to a high-fat diet 名词后缺后置分词\n【依据】实验术语：把受试者\"分到\"某组\n在临床/对照实验语境里 \"participants/adults assigned to … group/diet\" 是定式，意\n为\"被分配到 X 饮食组\"，与下文 compared with a low-fat diet control group（与低脂对\n照组比较）的实验设计完全吻合。\n【竞争词】F) designated \"指定\"重\"命名/身份\"，不用于分组\ndesignate 强调\"指定…为某身份/角色\"，如 designated driver；用于\"把受试者分到某饮食\"既\n不地道，也与实验术语的常规说法不符。", "points": ["选词填空", "动词"]}, {"id": "2019-12-1-c-32", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 32, "stem": "", "options": ["A) assessed", "B) assigned", "C) consequences", "D) conspicuously", "E) deficits", "F) designated", "G) detrimental", "H) digestion", "I) excelling", "J) indulging", "K) loopholes", "L) rapidly", "M) redundant", "N) regularly", "O) similar"], "answer": "O", "analysis": "【答案】O) similar\n形容词\n【词性槽】problems ... ___ to those observed in ... 后置定语缺形容词\n【依据】similar to 固定搭配 + 与对照实验语义贴合\nsimilar to those observed in overweight and obese individuals 意为\"与在超重和肥胖\n人群中观察到的相似\"，与本段\"短期高脂高糖饮食带来的认知损伤与长期肥胖者类似\"的实验\n结论完全吻合。", "points": ["选词填空", "形容词"]}, {"id": "2019-12-1-c-33", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 33, "stem": "", "options": ["A) assessed", "B) assigned", "C) consequences", "D) conspicuously", "E) deficits", "F) designated", "G) detrimental", "H) digestion", "I) excelling", "J) indulging", "K) loopholes", "L) rapidly", "M) redundant", "N) regularly", "O) similar"], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C) consequences\n名词\n【词性槽】negative ___ of ... 形容词后缺名词中心词\n【依据】与 negative 同款搭配，呼应全文\"差饮食的负面后果\"\nnegative consequences 是英语高频搭配，意为\"负面后果\"；本句正在总结前文：体重虽未\n明显变化，但差饮食的负面后果照样出现，consequences 与 of poor dietary intake、与\noverview 主旨完全对得上。\n【竞争词】H) digestion 缩小到\"消化\"，与主旨不符\n全文谈\"对大脑/认知的负面影响\"，把范围缩到\"消化\"反而偏离主线；且 negative digestion 不\n是地道搭配。\nK) loopholes 义为\"漏洞\"，与饮食危害无关\nloopholes 多指法律/规则漏洞，与\"差饮食的影响\"在语义场上风马牛不相及。\n【易错】最具迷惑性的错误词是 H) —— digestion 词性、与 diet 的话题相关性都合，但主线对不\n上。看到 of poor dietary intake，digestion 在话题上的\"相关感\"会诱使主体考生顺手就\n填；但全文论的是\"对大脑/认知的负面后果\"，consequences 才是覆盖全文负面影响的总\n称；防坑点：选名词不仅看搭配，还要看与全文主旨能否对齐。", "points": ["选词填空", "名词"]}, {"id": "2019-12-1-c-34", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 34, "stem": "", "options": ["A) assessed", "B) assigned", "C) consequences", "D) conspicuously", "E) deficits", "F) designated", "G) detrimental", "H) digestion", "I) excelling", "J) indulging", "K) loopholes", "L) rapidly", "M) redundant", "N) regularly", "O) similar"], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D) conspicuously\n副词\n【词性槽】has not changed ___ 完成时谓语后缺副词\n【依据】对应前句\"体重并无显著差异\"——\"体重没有显著变化\"\n本段开头 Body weight was not hugely different between the groups eating a healthy\ndiet and those on high fat and sugar diets.——\"体重并未有大的差异\"；此处 has not\nchanged conspicuously 正是\"没有显著地变化\"，与上文 not hugely different 同义呼\n应，强调即便体重看上去没明显变化，差饮食的负面后果照样发生。\n【竞争词】L) rapidly 义为\"迅速地\"，方向偏离段意\n本段重点不是\"变化快慢\"，而是\"变化是否显著\"；填 rapidly（\"没有迅速变化\"）就把原文\"体重\n并无显著差异\"的论证逻辑改成了\"变化速度\"，与上下文不接。\n【易错】最具迷惑性的错误词是 L) —— rapidly 词性也对、读着也通——但偏离段意。body\nweight has not changed __(34)__ 表面看 rapidly（没有迅速变化）能读通，但本段首句\nBody weight was not hugely different 已经定调是\"没有显著差异\"，conspicuously 才是\n同义回扣；防坑点：副词空一律回看本段首句找同义改写。", "points": ["选词填空", "副词"]}, {"id": "2019-12-1-c-35", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 35, "stem": "", "options": ["A) assessed", "B) assigned", "C) consequences", "D) conspicuously", "E) deficits", "F) designated", "G) detrimental", "H) digestion", "I) excelling", "J) indulging", "K) loopholes", "L) rapidly", "M) redundant", "N) regularly", "O) similar"], "answer": "N", "analysis": "【答案】N) regularly\n副词\n【词性槽】动词 exercise 后缺修饰语\n【依据】与 eat well 对仗，呼应\"健康生活方式\"\n本句在告诫\"瘦人也仍需好好吃、_____ 地锻炼\"，与全文\"健康习惯要长期坚持\"的主旨相合；\nregularly（有规律地）正是 exercise 的高频固定搭配——exercise regularly 是英语里\"规\n律锻炼\"的最常说法，和 eat well 一起组成完美的健康行为对仗。\n【竞争词】D) conspicuously 义为\"显眼地\"，与锻炼搭不上\nconspicuously 修饰的是\"显眼、引人注目\"，exercise conspicuously（显眼地锻炼）在语义上\n不成立，也与全文劝诫主旨毫不相干。\nL) rapidly 义为\"迅速地\"，搭配/语义不对\nrapidly 强调速度快，而本句的对仗目的是\"长期坚持\"而非\"快速\"；且 exercise rapidly 不是英语\n里的常见健康忠告搭配。", "points": ["选词填空", "副词"]}, {"id": "2019-12-1-m-36", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 36, "stem": "The year when most Americans began using smartphones was identified as a turning point in young Americans' level of happiness.", "options": null, "answer": "G", "analysis": "【答案】G 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】强锚点：smartphones + turning point\n【定位】It looks like it—after all, 2012 was the tipping point when more than half of Americans began\nowning smartphones.\n看起来确实如此——毕竟，2012 年是超过半数美国人开始拥有智能手机的临界点。\n【改写】The year when most Americans began using smartphones ↔\n2012 was the tipping point when more than half of Americans began owning smartphones\nmost ↔ more than half 同义；using ↔ owning 近义\na turning point in young Americans' level of happiness ↔ 2012 was the tipping point\nturning point ↔ tipping point 是同义替换\n【辨邻】 C 题干问的是“smartphones 过半的那一年是 happiness 的 turning point”，C 段只讲\n“2012 福祉指标开始下降”，没有把“那一年”与“smartphones 普及”或“turning point”\n绑定，因此只是话题相邻、并非源头段。\nBut around 2012 these measures started to decline", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2019-12-1-m-37", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 37, "stem": "Scores in various wellbeing measures began to go downward among young Americans in recent years.", "options": null, "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】强锚点：began to go downward + various wellbeing measures\n【定位】But around 2012 these measures started to decline. This continued through 2016, the most\nrecent year for which data is available.\n但大约在 2012 年，这些（福祉）指标开始下降。这一趋势一直延续到 2016 年，这是目前\n有数据可查的最近一年。\n【改写】Scores in various wellbeing measures ↔\nmeasures like self-esteem, life satisfaction, happiness and satisfaction with individual domains\n上位 various measures ↔ 下位列举具体指标\nbegan to go downward ↔ these measures started to decline\nbegan ↔ started、go downward ↔ decline 同义\nin recent years ↔\nBut around 2012 these measures started to decline. This continued through 2016, the most\nrecent year for which data is available\nrecent years ↔ 2012–2016 的具体年段\n【辨邻】 B B 段提到的福祉是上升趋势，且时间锚点是 1960s–early 2000s；题干要求的是“下降”\n且“近几年”，方向、时点都不匹配。\nmeasures of average wellbeing went up in the US", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2019-12-1-m-38", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 38, "stem": "Unfortunately, activities involving direct contact with people, which contributed to better wellbeing, were found to be on the decline.", "options": null, "answer": "H", "analysis": "【答案】H 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】强锚点：direct contact with people + on the decline\n【定位】Sadly, the data also showed face-to-face socializing and sports activity had declined over the\nperiod covered by the survey.\n遗憾的是，数据还显示面对面社交与体育活动在调查所覆盖的时期内有所下降。\n【改写】activities involving direct contact with people ↔ face-to-face socializing and sports activity\ndirect contact ↔ face-to-face 同义\nwhich contributed to better wellbeing ↔\nyears when people spent more time with friends were better years for wellbeing\n活动多 → 福祉更好，是 contributed 的来源\nwere found to be on the decline ↔ had declined over the period covered by the survey\nhad declined ↔ on the decline 名词化改写\n【辨邻】 E 题干强调的是“这些活动 were found to be on the decline”，E 段给的是“做少了相关\n于福祉低”，没有断言这些活动整体在下降——这一关键趋势信息只在 H 段。\nLess sport, and fewer meetings with peers correlated with lower wellbeing", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2019-12-1-m-39", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 39, "stem": "In response to past critics, Twenge and her co-researchers stress they are not trying to prove that the use of digital devices reduces young people's wellbeing.", "options": null, "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】强锚点：Twenge + not trying to prove\n【定位】This time around, Twenge and her team make a point of saying that they are not trying to\nestablish causes as such, but that they are assessing the plausibility of potential causes.\n这一次，Twenge 及其团队特意声明，他们不是在确立因果关系本身，而是在评估各种潜在\n原因的合理性。\n【改写】In response to past critics ↔\nsome commentators were quick to raise this problem. They argued that her causal-sounding\nclaims rested on correlational data\npast critics ↔ some commentators 同义指代\nstress they are not trying to prove ↔\nmake a point of saying that they are not trying to establish causes as such\nstress ↔ make a point of saying；prove ↔ establish 同义\nthe use of digital devices reduces young people's wellbeing ↔ establish causes as such\n题干具体化原文的笼统 causes\n【辨邻】 J J 段虽也讲 Twenge 团队与 screen time，但讲的是“结论 + 新研究比以往更进一步”，\n没有“明确声明不在证因果”的立场转换。\nThe researchers conclude that electronic communication was the only adolescent activity\nthat increased", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2019-12-1-m-40", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 40, "stem": "In the last few decades of the 20th century, living standards went up and economic depressions were largely averted in the US.", "options": null, "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】强锚点：last few decades of the 20th century + economic depressions averted\n【定位】It reflected the fact that these decades saw a climb in general standards of living and\navoidance of mass societal traumas like full-scale war or economic deprivation.\n这反映了一个事实：这些数十年里，整体生活水平不断上升，并避开了像全面战争或经济剥\n夺这样的大规模社会创伤。\n【改写】In the last few decades of the 20th century ↔ from the 1960s into the early 2000s\n20 世纪最后几十年 ↔ 1960s 起的时段\nliving standards went up ↔ a climb in general standards of living\ngo up ↔ climb 同义；动词 vs. 名词化\neconomic depressions were largely averted ↔\navoidance of mass societal traumas like full-scale war or economic deprivation\naverted ↔ avoidance；economic depressions ↔ economic deprivation\n【辨邻】 I 题干要的是“上升 + 萧条被避开”的乐观叙事，I 段反而是“大衰退发生 + 其影响是否到\n福祉”，时段不在 20 世纪，立意也是负面事件而非正面避开，差异显著。\nthe impact of the great recession of 2007-2009", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2019-12-1-m-41", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 41, "stem": "Contrary to popular belief, doing homework might add to students' wellbeing.", "options": null, "answer": "E", "analysis": "【答案】E 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】强锚点：doing homework + 反直觉\n【定位】(This last finding would appear to contradict another popular hypothesis that it is our\nburdening of students with assignments that is causing all the problems.)\n（这最后一项发现似乎与另一个流行假说相矛盾——那个假说认为，正是给学生布置作业的\n重负在引发所有问题。）\n【改写】Contrary to popular belief ↔ would appear to contradict another popular hypothesis\ncontrary to popular belief ↔ contradict popular hypothesis 同义结构\ndoing homework might add to students' wellbeing ↔\nless time doing homework correlated with lower wellbeing\n反向推理：少做作业相关于低福祉 = 做作业利好福祉\npopular belief ↔\nit is our burdening of students with assignments that is causing all the problems\n通俗看法的具体内容即“作业过重 = 万恶之源”\n【辨邻】 F F 段与题干都涉及福祉，但讨论的影响因素是屏幕时间而非作业，不能命中要求\nhomework 的题。\ndigital devices—a couple of hours a week—had the highest wellbeing", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2019-12-1-m-42", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 42, "stem": "The author believes the researchers' new study has gone a step further regarding the impact of screen time on wellbeing.", "options": null, "answer": "J", "analysis": "【答案】J 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】强锚点：author believes + gone a step further\n【定位】But the new work does go further than previous research and suggests that screen time should\nstill be considered a potential barrier to young people's flourishing.\n但这项新研究确实比以往的研究更进一步，并提示屏幕时间仍应被视为青少年发展繁荣的潜\n在障碍。\n【改写】The author believes ↔\nI suspect that some experts in the field will be keen to address alternative explanations\n原文用 I suspect 标明作者第一人称视角\nhas gone a step further regarding the impact of screen time on wellbeing ↔\nthe new work does go further than previous research and suggests that screen time should still\nbe considered a potential barrier to young people's flourishing\ngone a step further ↔ go further than previous research；screen time on wellbeing ↔\nscreen time as barrier to flourishing\n【辨邻】 D D 段主语是 Twenge and her team（研究者），J 段才出现 I suspect / new work\ndoes go further 这种作者主语；题干要的是后者。\nTwenge and her team make a point of saying that they are not trying to establish causes as such", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2019-12-1-m-43", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 43, "stem": "The researchers found that extended screen time makes young people less happy.", "options": null, "answer": "F", "analysis": "【答案】F 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】强锚点：extended screen time + less happy\n【定位】However, higher doses of screen time were clearly associated with lower happiness.\n然而，更高剂量的屏幕时间明显与更低的快乐感相关。\n【改写】extended screen time ↔ higher doses of screen time\nextended ↔ higher doses 同义\nmakes young people less happy ↔ were clearly associated with lower happiness\nless happy ↔ lower happiness 同义；makes ↔ associated with 强弱差异需注意\nless happy ↔\nThose spending 10-19 hours per week on their devices were 41 percent more likely to be\nunhappy\n百分比数据支撑“剂量 ↔ 不快乐”的断言\n【辨邻】 G G 段粒度是“年份层面”的同步追踪，F 段才是“个体层面”的剂量-不开心关系。题干\nextended screen time makes young people less happy 描述的是个体剂量效应，\n归属 F 段。\nwellbeing was indeed lowest in years where adolescents spent more time online\nJ J 段给的是“电子通讯增加 + 福祉下降”的同步关系，题干则在问“剂量延长 → 不开心”\n这种个体层面剂量效应，最匹配仍是 F 段。\nelectronic communication was the only adolescent activity that increased at the same time\npsychological wellbeing declined", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2019-12-1-m-44", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 44, "stem": "Data reveals that economic inequality rather than family income might affect people's wellbeing.", "options": null, "answer": "I", "analysis": "【答案】I 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】强锚点：economic inequality + family income 对比\n【定位】They found some evidence that some crude measures, like income inequality, correlated\nwith changes in wellbeing, but economic measures with a more direct impact, like family\nincome and unemployment rates (which put families into difficulties), had no relationship\nwith wellbeing.\n他们发现了一些证据：像收入不平等这样的粗略指标与福祉变化相关，但更直接影响家庭的\n经济指标，如家庭收入与失业率，却与福祉没有关系。\n【改写】economic inequality ↔ income inequality\neconomic inequality ↔ income inequality 同义\nrather than family income ↔\nfamily income and unemployment rates ... had no relationship with wellbeing\nrather than ↔ 原文“前者相关、后者无关”对比\nmight affect people's wellbeing ↔ correlated with changes in wellbeing\nmight affect ↔ correlated with 弱化等值\n【辨邻】 B B 段虽提及经济议题，但缺失题干要求的“economic inequality vs. family income”的\n对比要素，故仅话题相邻。\navoidance of mass societal traumas like full-scale war or economic deprivation", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2019-12-1-m-45", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 45, "stem": "Too much screen time is widely believed to be the cause of unhappiness among today's young people.", "options": null, "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】强锚点：widely believed to be the cause + 通俗看法\n【定位】And one of the most popular explanations, among some experts and the popular media, is that\nexcessive \"screen time\" is to blame.\n并且，在一些专家和大众媒体中，最流行的解释之一是：过度的“屏幕时间”是罪魁祸首。\n【改写】Too much screen time ↔ excessive \"screen time\"\ntoo much ↔ excessive 同义\nwidely believed to be the cause ↔\none of the most popular explanations, among some experts and the popular media, is that\nexcessive \"screen time\" is to blame\nwidely believed ↔ popular explanations / is to blame ↔ cause\nunhappiness among today's young people ↔ all is not well for our youth\nall is not well ↔ unhappiness 婉转表达\n【辨邻】 J 题干 widely believed 指公众/媒体的流行看法，源自开篇引出议题的 A 段；J 段是末\n尾研究者结论，性质不同。\nscreen time should still be considered a potential barrier to young people's flourishing", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2019-12-1-r-46", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 46, "stem": "Why do some experts consider lying a milestone in a child's development?", "options": ["A) It shows they have the ability to view complex situations from different angles.", "B) It indicates they have an ability more remarkable than crawling and walking.", "C) It represents their ability to actively interact with people around them.", "D) It involves the coordination of both their mental and physical abilities."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题）\n【判型】题干 Why do some experts consider lying a milestone in a child's development? 是问\n「为什么把撒谎视作里程碑」，问的是原文已经写明的事实理由，不是推断。\n【拆句】主干是 Why do some experts consider lying a milestone，consider A B 是「认为 A 是\nB」的双宾结构；介词短语 in a child's development 修饰 milestone，限定语义场为儿童\n发展，结构平铺无陷阱。\n【定位】Some experts even consider lying a developmental milestone, like crawling and walking,\nbecause it requires sophisticated planning, attention and the ability to see a situation from\nsomeone else's perspective to manipulate them.\n一些专家甚至将撒谎视为一种发育里程碑，类似爬行和走路，因为它需要复杂的规划、注意\n力，以及从他人视角看待情境以操纵对方的能力。\n【选项】A) ✓ 正确\nthe ability to see a situation from someone else's perspective ↔\nthe ability to view complex situations from different angles\nsophisticated planning, attention and the ability to see a situation from someone else's\nperspective\n↔ complex situations from different angles\n预判要的是「综合高级思维」，A 项 view complex situations from different angles\n把原文 see a situation from someone else's perspective 精确改写出来，命中预判。\nB) 偷换概念\n原文用 like crawling and walking 把撒谎与爬行、走路并列为发育里程碑，并未说撒谎\n「比 more remarkable than crawling and walking」。\nC) 偷换概念\n原文讲 see a situation from someone else's perspective to manipulate them，重\n点是「换位思考以操纵」，而非积极地 actively interact with people；选项把「操纵」\n美化为「互动」。\nD) 无中生有\n原文列举的三项 sophisticated planning, attention and the ability to see a situation\nfrom someone else's perspective 全是心智活动，不含体力活动；选项的 both their\nmental and physical abilities 凭空多出 physical 一维。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2019-12-1-r-47", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 47, "stem": "Why does the Harvard neuroscientist say that lying takes work?", "options": ["A) It is hard to choose from several options.", "B) It is difficult to sound natural or plausible.", "C) It requires speedy blood flow into one's brain.", "D) It involves lots of sophisticated mental activity."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题）\n【判型】题干 Why does the Harvard neuroscientist say that lying takes work? 问神经科学家为\n什么作此判断，理由原文必有交代。\n【拆句】主干 Why does the Harvard neuroscientist say...，that 引导宾语从句 that lying takes\nwork，takes work 这里取「需要付出努力/费劲」的引申义，不是字面的「带去工作」，是\n典型的熟词僻义点。\n【定位】Harvard cognitive neuroscientist Joshua Greene says, for most of us, lying takes work. In\nstudies, he gave subjects a chance to deceive for monetary gain while examining their brains\nin a functional MRI machine, which maps blood flow to active parts of the brain. Some\npeople told the truth instantly and instinctively. But others opted to lie, and they showed\nincreased activity in their frontal parietal(颅腔壁的) control network, which is involved in\ndifficult or complex thinking.\n哈佛认知神经科学家 Joshua Greene 说，对大多数人而言，撒谎是要费劲的。在研究中，\n他让受试者有机会为金钱回报而撒谎，同时用 fMRI 检查他们的大脑——fMRI 能映射出大\n脑活跃区域的血流。有些人会即时本能地说真话，但另一些人选择撒谎，他们的颅腔壁控制\n网络活动增强，而这一区域涉及困难或复杂的思考。\n【选项】A) 偷换概念\n原文 deciding between truth and dishonesty 是说撒谎者在「说真说假」之间挣扎，\n并未说选项 hard to choose from several options 的「多项中难选」；A 把二选一夸\n大成多选一，且重点偏离了「费劲」的脑科学解释。\nB) 无中生有\n原文讨论的是撒谎时大脑活动加剧，没说撒谎者要 sound natural or plausible（让谎\n言听起来自然可信）；这种表达层面的难度是常识联想，原文未涉及。\nC) 偷换概念\n原文是说 fMRI 仪器 maps blood flow to active parts of the brain——把血流映射到\n活跃脑区作观察手段，并非撒谎本身需要 speedy blood flow into one's brain。选项\n把仪器原理偷换成撒谎的生理条件。\nD) ✓ 正确\nwhich is involved in difficult or complex thinking ↔ lots of sophisticated mental activity\nincreased activity in their frontal parietal(颅腔壁的) control network ↔\ninvolves lots of sophisticated mental activity\n预判要的是「撒谎牵涉复杂脑活动」，D 项 involves lots of sophisticated mental\nactivity 把原文 complex thinking 用 sophisticated mental activity 精确改写，命中\n预判。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2019-12-1-r-48", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 48, "stem": "Under what circumstances do people tend to lie?", "options": ["A) When they become too emotional.", "B) When they face too much peer pressure.", "C) When the temptation is too strong.", "D) When the consequences are not imminent."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题）\n【判型】题干 Under what circumstances do people tend to lie? 问的是原文列举过的具体条件之\n一，答案就在原文的条件清单里。\n【拆句】Under what circumstances 作状语前置，主干 do people tend to lie；tend to do 「倾\n向于…」，全句无修饰从句，结构平铺无陷阱。\n【定位】We are more likely to lie, when we are able to rationalise it, when we are stressed and\nfatigued or see others being dishonest.\n我们更容易撒谎——当我们能把它合理化时，当我们承受压力或疲惫时，或者当我们看到别\n人也不诚实时。\n【选项】A) 偷换概念\n原文条件清单是 able to rationalise it、stressed and fatigued、see others being\ndishonest，没有提到 become too emotional（变得过于情绪化）。stressed 与\nemotional 不是同义概念，前者特指压力疲劳，后者指情绪失控。\nB) 偷换概念\n原文 see others being dishonest 是「看到别人也不诚实」，重点在「他人行为示范」\n而非 peer pressure（同辈群体压力要求一致行为）。前者是观察学习，后者是群体\n强制。\nC) ✓ 正确\nwe are able to rationalise it ↔ the temptation is too strong\nthe inability to resist temptation ↔ the temptation is too strong\n在四个选项里，只有 C 项 the temptation is too strong 能与「rationalise it」「无法\n抗拒诱惑」这条主线对得上；A/B/D 都不在原文条件清单内，故锁定 C。\nD) 张冠李戴\n原文条件清单里没有 consequences are not imminent（后果不即将到来）这一条；与\n「后果」相关的内容在第四段讲「不受惩罚谎言会变大」（属 50 题），但那是「不受罚\n的结果」，不是「撒谎的触发条件」。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2019-12-1-r-49", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 49, "stem": "When are people less likely to lie?", "options": ["A) When they are worn out and stressed.", "B) When they are under watchful eyes.", "C) When they think in a rational way.", "D) When they have a clear conscience."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题）\n【判型】题干 When are people less likely to lie? 问的是原文列举过的「不易撒谎」的条件，答案\n是原文事实陈述，不需推断。\n【拆句】When 引导状语从句省略形式，主干 are people less likely to lie；less likely 是 likely 的\n比较级形式，构成反向倾向。\n【定位】And we are less likely to lie when we have moral reminders or when we think others\nare watching.\n当我们有道德提醒、或者认为别人正在注视着我们时，我们就不那么容易撒谎。\n【选项】A) 与原文相反\n原文 we are stressed and fatigued 出现在 more likely to lie 的条件里——「压力疲\n惫」是「更易撒谎」的条件，而非「不易撒谎」。选项 worn out and stressed 把它搬\n到「less likely」侧，正反弄反。\nB) ✓ 正确\nwe think others are watching ↔ under watchful eyes\n预判要的是「认为有人在看」的状态，B 项 under watchful eyes 用名词化短语精确改\n写了 think others are watching，是同义替换中典型的「动词从句→名词短语」转化，\n命中预判。\nC) 偷换概念\nthink in a rational way（理性思考）与原文 rationalise it（合理化撒谎）措辞相近、语\n义却相反——前者是冷静客观，后者是为撒谎找借口；并且 rationalise 在原文是 more\nlikely 一侧的条件。\nD) 偷换概念\n原文 moral reminders 是「外部的道德提醒/警示」（如海报、宣誓），不是 a clear\nconscience（自己问心无愧）。前者是外来刺激，后者是内心状态，且原文从未涉及\n「良心是否干净」的判断。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2019-12-1-r-50", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 50, "stem": "What does the author say will happen when a liar does not get punished?", "options": ["A) They may feel justified.", "B) They will tell bigger lies.", "C) They will become complacent.", "D) They may mix lies and truths."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题）\n【判型】题干 What does the author say will happen when a liar does not get punished? 问的\n是原文交代过的后果，答案直接陈述于文中。\n【拆句】主干 What does the author say will happen，What 是 will happen 的主语，作 say 的\n宾语从句的主句主语；when a liar does not get punished 是时间状语从句，整句虽嵌套\n但语义清晰。\n【定位】Not only that, but when people faced no consequences for dishonesty, their falsehood tended\nto get even more sensational. This means that if you give people multiple opportunities to lie\nfor their own benefit, they start with little lies which get bigger over time.\n不仅如此，当人们因撒谎而不受任何后果时，他们的谎言会变得越发耸人听闻。这意味着，\n如果你给人们多次为自身利益撒谎的机会，他们会从小谎开始，谎言会随时间越变越大。\n【选项】A) 过度推断\n原文谈的是 negative signals from the amygdala began to decrease——脑区负面信\n号减弱（是脑层面的反应），并未说撒谎者 feel justified（觉得自己有理、问心无\n愧）。脑信号减弱不等同于「合理化」心理。\nB) ✓ 正确\nthey start with little lies which get bigger over time ↔ tell bigger lies\ntheir falsehood tended to get even more sensational ↔ They will tell bigger lies\n预判要的是「谎言规模升级」，B 项 tell bigger lies 把原文「越发耸人听闻」与「小谎\n变大谎」两条线索精确浓缩为一句，命中预判。\nC) 无中生有\n原文没有写撒谎者会 become complacent（自满）；自满是一种心理态度，原文谈的\n是「谎言规模」与「脑信号」，不是态度上的自得。\nD) 无中生有\n原文讲的是「谎言越编越大」，并未说撒谎者会 mix lies and truths（把真话假话混在\n一起）；前者是规模升级，后者是策略变化，方向不同。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2019-12-1-r-51", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 51, "stem": "What does \"The Big One\" refer to?", "options": ["A) A gigantic geological fault.", "B) A large-scale exercise to prepare for disasters.", "C) A massive natural catastrophe.", "D) A huge tsunami on the Caledonia coast."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题）\n【判型】What does \"The Big One\" refer to? 问的是一个具体称呼的所指，答案就在原文释义里。\n【拆句】句式 What does \"The Big One\" refer to? 为简单 wh- 疑问句，主语 \"The Big One\" 借助\n助动词 does 倒装，refer to 是动词短语；结构平铺直叙，无陷阱。\n【定位】Here's how the Pacific Northwest is preparing for \"The Big One\". It's the mother of all\ndisaster drills for what could be the worst disaster in American history.\n下面是太平洋西北部如何为 \"The Big One\" 做准备。这是为可能成为美国历史上最严重灾难\n的一场\"灾难演练之母\"。\n【选项】A) 偷换概念\n选项把 \"The Big One\" 偷换为 A gigantic geological fault；原文虽提到 San Andreas\n/ Cascadia 断层，但 \"The Big One\" 指的是断层引发的灾难，而非断层本身。\nB) 偷换概念\nA large-scale exercise to prepare for disasters 描述的是 disaster drills 这件事，而\n\"The Big One\" 指的是\"将要应对的灾难\"，两者是\"演练 vs. 演练所应对的灾难\"，正好被\n偷换。\nC) ✓ 正确\nthe worst disaster in American history ↔ A massive natural catastrophe\n预判结论是\"最严重的自然灾难\"，四个选项中只有 A massive natural catastrophe 与\n之同义对应，其他三项指向断层、演练或海啸（地点错），均偏离释义。\nD) 无中生有\n原文反复出现的是 Cascadia，从未出现 Caledonia；选项 A huge tsunami on the\nCaledonia coast 用形近词制造混淆，同时把灾难范围缩小为\"海啸\"，缩窄并改地名。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2019-12-1-r-52", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 52, "stem": "What is the purpose of the Cascadia Rising drill?", "options": ["A) To prepare people for a major earthquake and tsunami.", "B) To increase residents' awareness of imminent disasters.", "C) To teach people how to adapt to post-disaster life.", "D) To cope with the aftermath of a possible earthquake."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题）\n【判型】What is the purpose of the Cascadia Rising drill? 直接询问某个具体演练的目的，答案\n应在原文显式给出。\n【拆句】句式 What is the purpose of the Cascadia Rising drill? 为 What + be + 主语 的简单疑\n问句，主语是 the purpose of the Cascadia Rising drill，结构清晰、无歧义。\n【定位】The Cascadia Rising drill gives area residents and emergency responders a chance to practice\nwhat to do in case of a 9.0 magnitude earthquake and tsunami along one of the nation's\ndangerous—and underestimated—faults.\nCascadia Rising 演练让本地居民与应急响应人员有机会演练：当沿一条全国最危险且被低\n估的断层发生 9.0 级地震和海啸时该怎么办。\n【选项】A) ✓ 正确\npractice what to do in case of a 9.0 magnitude earthquake and tsunami ↔\nTo prepare people for a major earthquake and tsunami\n预判核心是\"为地震和海啸做准备\"，四项里只有 A 同时含 prepare、earthquake、\ntsunami 三要素，且对象 people 与原文听众范围吻合；其他三项要么换动作、要么换\n对象、要么换时机。\nB) 偷换概念\nTo increase residents' awareness of imminent disasters 把 practice 偷换为\nincrease...awareness；原文目的是\"练习如何应对\"这一动作，而非\"提升意识\"这一心理\n状态；且 imminent 在原文无依据。\nC) 偷换概念\nTo teach people how to adapt to post-disaster life 谈灾后生活适应，与原文 in case\nof...earthquake and tsunami 谈的灾时应对完全错位；演练发生在灾前，目标动作是灾\n时反应，不是灾后适应。\nD) 以偏概全\nTo cope with the aftermath of a possible earthquake 中 aftermath（后果/余波）\n指事后处理，与原文\"事中应对\"错位；且漏掉了 tsunami 这一并列灾种，缩小了演练\n范围。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2019-12-1-r-53", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 53, "stem": "What happens in case of a megathrust earthquake according to the passage?", "options": ["A) Two plates merge into one.", "B) A variety of forces converge.", "C) Boundaries blur between plates.", "D) Enormous stress is released."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题）\n【判型】What happens in case of a megathrust earthquake according to the passage? 加了\naccording to the passage 直接指明答案在原文有据可查。\n【拆句】句式 What happens in case of a megathrust earthquake according to the passage?\n主干 What happens，in case of a megathrust earthquake 是表条件的介词短语，\naccording to the passage 是范围状语；in case of 这里相当于 \"在...情况下\"，与 if 同义，\n需要识破熟词僻义。\n【定位】During a megathrust event, all of that stress releases and some of the world's most powerful\nearthquakes occur.\n在一次巨型逆冲（megathrust）事件中，所有累积的应力一次释放，世界上一些最强的地\n震就此发生。\n【选项】A) 过度推断\nTwo plates merge into one 把原文 two plates converge 拔高为 merge into one\n（合并成一块），但 converge 只是 \"在边界处相遇\"，并未真合并；且原文 one plate\nis beneath another 明示一块在另一块下方，并非融合。\nB) 张冠李戴\nA variety of forces converge 把原文唯一的主体 stress 替换成 forces，并把\nconverge 错位安到 forces 头上；原文 converge 描述的是 plates 而非 forces，stress\n才是被释放的对象。\nC) 无中生有\nBoundaries blur between plates 描述的是 \"板块边界变模糊\"，原文 land plate\nboundaries where two plates converge 只说 \"板块边界处两板块汇聚\"，并未提到边\n界变模糊，属凭空造词。\nD) ✓ 正确\nall of that stress releases ↔ Enormous stress is released\n四个选项中只有 D 同时含 stress 与 released 两个核心元素，正好对应原文 all of that\nstress releases；其余三项要么改主语（plates/forces/boundaries），要么改动词\n（merge/converge/blur），都没击中过程动作核心。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2019-12-1-r-54", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 54, "stem": "What do the officials hope to achieve through the drills?", "options": ["A) Coordinating various disaster-relief efforts.", "B) Reducing casualties in the event of a disaster.", "C) Minimizing property loss caused by disaster.", "D) Establishing disaster and emergency management."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题）\n【判型】What do the officials hope to achieve through the drills? 是对原文具体表态句的事实复\n述，答案在原文官员发声句里。\n【拆句】句式 What do the officials hope to achieve through the drills? 主干 the officials hope\nto achieve what，疑问词 what 前置；through the drills 是表方式的介词短语，明确限定\n\"借助演练\" 这个手段。\n【定位】Not all casualties can necessarily be prevented—but by coordinating across local, state, and\neven national borders, officials hope that the worst-case scenario can be averted.\n并非所有伤亡都能被避免——但通过在地方、州乃至跨国层面进行协调，官员们希望能避免\n最坏的情况发生。\n【选项】A) 偷换概念\nCoordinating various disaster-relief efforts 把原文 by coordinating across local,\nstate, and even national borders 即\"协调\"这一手段当作目标——但原文用 by 引出协\n调，是手段；用 hope that 引出 averted，才是目标。\nB) ✓ 正确\nNot all casualties can necessarily be prevented ↔\nReducing casualties in the event of a disaster\nthe worst-case scenario can be averted ↔ in the event of a disaster\n预判核心是\"在灾难发生时减少伤亡\"，四项中只有 B 同时含 reduce / casualties /\ndisaster 三要素；A 强调过程而非结果，C 替换为财产损失（原文未提），D 是建立体\n系（拔高）。\nC) 无中生有\nMinimizing property loss caused by disaster 把原文 casualties（伤亡）替换为\nproperty loss（财产损失），但全文从未谈财产损失，只谈人员伤亡（Thousands\nof casualties）。\nD) 过度推断\nEstablishing disaster and emergency management 是说要\"建立\"灾害与应急管理体\n系——原文 the report they prepare during this rehearsal will inform disaster\nmanagement for years to come 只说报告将\"为未来的应急管理提供参考\n（inform）\"，并未说要从零建立；inform ≠ establish。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2019-12-1-r-55", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 55, "stem": "What does the author say about \"The Big One\"?", "options": ["A) Whether it will occur remains to be seen.", "B) How it will arrive is too early to predict.", "C) Its occurrence is just a matter of time.", "D) It keeps haunting Cascadia residents."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题）\n【判型】What does the author say about \"The Big One\"? 问的是作者明示态度，原文末段有直接\n陈述，无须推断。\n【拆句】句式 What does the author say about \"The Big One\"? 简单 wh- 疑问句，say about X\n表 \"就 X 谈了什么\"；介词 about 引出谈论对象，结构无陷阱。\n【定位】For hundreds of thousands of Cascadia residents, \"The Big One\" isn't a question of if,\nonly when.\n对成千上万 Cascadia 居民来说，\"The Big One\" 不是\"会不会发生\"的问题，而只是\"何时发\n生\"的问题。\n【选项】A) 与原文相反\nWhether it will occur remains to be seen 等于说 \"是否发生还说不准\"，正好是原文\nisn't a question of if 所明确否定的态度——原文恰恰认为 \"if\" 已不是问题。\nB) 偷换概念\nHow it will arrive is too early to predict 把焦点换成\"如何到来\"，并断言\"现在预测还\n太早\"。原文谈的是\"何时（when）\"且明确说一定会来，并没说 how；且末段最后一句\nit's never too early to get ready for the inevitable 反对 too early 这一立场。\nC) ✓ 正确\nisn't a question of if, only when ↔ Its occurrence is just a matter of time\n预判核心是\"必然发生，只是时间问题\"，四项中只有 C 同时含\"occurrence（必然性默\n认）\"与\"matter of time（时间问题）\"两要素；其他三项把\"是否\"或\"如何\"留作未解，\n与原文断言相反。\nD) 无中生有\nIt keeps haunting Cascadia residents 用 haunt（萦绕、纠缠不去）描写居民的心理\n被困扰，原文只是冷静陈述 \"对居民而言这是个迟早问题\"，并没说居民被困扰，属心理\n状态的无中生有。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}]});
